rivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-5}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-5)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{125}{3} \\\\  & =63.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186064737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062449 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186064737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297024 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186064737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186064737 1071 "$\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186064737 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int - 2e^{x}dx$?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186064737 1074 "Factoring out the constant $-2$ and calculating the resulting integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297232 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297305 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068692 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297231 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297349 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297365 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297397 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297398 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297443 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297444 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297447 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068695 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297399 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068695 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297400 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068695 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297439 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297354 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297405 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297423 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297261 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297262 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297408 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297332 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297341 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297378 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297401 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297407 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297264 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297265 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186068923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297293 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066564 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296763 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069236 1071 "![](images/11/1126d885ff5000b4afdadffef7b47dec3b2932322dfa7b75d16bc00993218a0e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $- (x - 2)(x - 4) > 0$." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069236 1071 "![](images/11/1126d885ff5000b4afdadffef7b47dec3b2932322dfa7b75d16bc00993218a0e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-2)(x-4)>0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069236 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-2)(x-4)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x-2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc39c970e292e38519e609367c9f4a3d810aaca99e7040bb6227ff45401b2efc.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2<x<4.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066588 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297135 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069240 1071 "For the curve $y = \\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} + \\frac{4}{3}$, find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069240 1071 "For the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069240 1074 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{4}{3}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=2.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,2).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066658 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296859 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069253 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069253 1071 "What is $lim_(x → 0)(x\\cos x)/(\\ln (x + 1))$?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069253 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(\\frac{1}{x+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{(\\frac{1}{0+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066664 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296654 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069254 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186069254 1071 "What is $lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{2\\ln x}{e^{x}}$?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186069254 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186078391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297371 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297370 13194139533411 17592