of the solutions equals $-5.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083652 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=3.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{b}{3} \\\\ -12 & =b \\\\ b & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-5.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -5 & =\\frac{c}{3} \\\\ -15 & =c \\\\ c & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=3,\\,b=-12,\\,c=-15\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-12x-15=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080846 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296669 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083655 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+7x-27=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083655 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n$9x^{2} + 7x - 27 = 0$.\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083655 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\,b=7,\\,c=-27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-27)}{9}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080852 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296647 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083656 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-312x+22\\,464=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083656 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n$x^{2} - 312x + 22464 = 0$.\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083656 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-312,\\,c=22\\,464.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{22\\,464}{1}=22\\,464.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080858 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296821 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083657 1071 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1$. The sum of the solutions equals $7$, and the product of the solutions equals $12$. Find the equation.The equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$= 0$." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083657 1071 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1.$ The sum of the solutions equals $7,$ and the product of the solutions equals $12.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083657 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=1.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $7.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{b}{1} \\\\ -7 & =b \\\\ b & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $12.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{c}{1} \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-7,\\,c=12\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080883 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296804 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083662 1071 "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3/2}+4{t}^{2},\\,11t-1⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083662 1071 "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n$r = ⟨t^{3/2} + 4t^{2}, 11t - 1⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083662 1074 "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3/2}+4{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(11t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}+8t,\\,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080889 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296891 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083663 1071 "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t,\\,2{t}^{2}-4t+2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186083663 1071 "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n$r = ⟨t^{3} - 2t^{2} - t, 2t^{2} - 4t + 2⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t = 2$." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186083663 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-4t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-4t-1,4t-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨3(2)^{2}-4(2)-1,4(2)-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297549 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297553 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297386 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297490 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087746 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297491 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297533 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297534 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297535 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297536 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297537 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297540 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297541 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297544 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087802 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297542 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087802 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297546 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297469 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297489 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297526 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297527 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297528 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297529 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297530 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297531 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297532 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297285 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297311 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297236 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297426 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297427 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186087896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297432 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085405 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186088132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296836 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088132 1071 "For the curve $y = 5(6^{x}) + 2$, find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186088132 1071 "For the curve $y=5({6}^{x})+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088132 1074 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={6}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=5({6}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=5+2=7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5({6}^{0})+2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =5+2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=5({6}^{x})+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,7).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085426 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186088138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296977 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088138 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨4t+4,6t-21⟩\\,,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨42,\\,-18⟩$ when $t=4,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186088138 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$a(t) = ⟨4t + 4, 6t - 21⟩, t > 0$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v = ⟨42,-18⟩$ when $t = 4$, calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186088138 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4t+4,6t-21⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (6t-21)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}+4t+{C}_{1},\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨42,\\,-18⟩.$ Substituting $t=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =⟨2(4)^{2}+4(4)+{C}_{1},\\,3(4)^{2}-21(4)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨42,\\,-18⟩ & =⟨48+{C}_{1},\\,-36+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=18.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{2}+4t-6,\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+18⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2}+4t-6,\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+18⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+4t-6=2(t-1)(t+3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-3,1, \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-21t+18=3(t-1)(t-6) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092187 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297436 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297507 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297508 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297509 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297510 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297511 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297512 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297513 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297514 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297515 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297516 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297517 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297518 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297519 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297520 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297521 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297522 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297523 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297524 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092234 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297525 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092267 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297346 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186092269 1038 #edb/ref 175921862