5585 false] [17592186097216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297124 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097216 1071 "![](images/04/04e636bd831dbfd45ebed1bc8928c4b68d479a1147b98474c667b2aee7a5efc1.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x) =-x^{3} + 2x + 1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186097216 1071 "![](images/04/04e636bd831dbfd45ebed1bc8928c4b68d479a1147b98474c667b2aee7a5efc1.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097216 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x+1$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095201 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186097217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297035 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097217 1071 "![](images/bb/bb9db2eacfc01d00ca8a3436f9cd416afda7b400ae2aa2718d4ce049cd36a381.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x) = x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186097217 1071 "![](images/bb/bb9db2eacfc01d00ca8a3436f9cd416afda7b400ae2aa2718d4ce049cd36a381.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097217 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3$ between the lower limit $x=-1$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095231 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186097222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297052 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097222 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097222 1071 "$\\sin x + \\sqrt{3}\\cos x =$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186097222 1074 "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095237 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186097223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296948 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097223 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186097223 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}e^{x/3}dx$?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186097223 1074 "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{x/3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297446 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100820 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297449 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297388 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297385 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297387 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297390 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297391 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297392 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297460 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297478 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297484 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297488 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297538 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297263 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297333 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100984 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297274 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186100986 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297275 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297306 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297307 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297316 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297317 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297464 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297310 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297465 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101029 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297466 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297467 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101030 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297468 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099373 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297019 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101368 1071 "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\csc x = 2$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101368 1071 "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101368 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/97/978eaf470ef3b61ff7839471ed4ac46f320922b4d30f7f95566095124d0526d7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099391 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296784 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101371 1071 "What is the average value of $h(x) = \\sin 2x$ on the interval $[\\frac{π}{4}, \\frac{π}{2}]$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101371 1071 "What is the average value of $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101371 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099413 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296950 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101377 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101377 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\log_{2} x}{x}dx$?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101377 1074 "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099421 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296940 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101380 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186101380 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int(\\log_{3} (x^{2}))/(x)dx$?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 false] [17592186101380 1074 "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2}).$Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\int u\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186105706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297267 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186105706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297268 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186105706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297273 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186105707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186105707 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297486 13194139533411 175