rue] [17592186297161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296205 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297161 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296555 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297161 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297161 1077 "selection" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297161 1079 #edb/ref 17592186296555 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297161 1080 #uuid "fe39cd51-4508-5a79-ba59-d1e9f1443e16" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297018 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297162 1069 "q-28341" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297162 1071 "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $16x+4y=12?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297162 1072 #uuid "ab7f99bc-8f2b-5a80-8f9e-dc70e299882e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297162 1074 "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $16x+4y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16x+4y & =12 \\\\ 4y & =-16x+12 \\\\ y & =-4x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-4.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ is perpendicular to the given line." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296777 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297163 1069 "q-28519" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297163 1071 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}.$ What is the other root?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297163 1072 #uuid "179b51f5-6286-53a0-bd97-91549ab321d7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297163 1074 "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=\\frac{-2+7\\text{i}}{2}=-1+\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=-1-\\frac{7}{2}\\text{i}=\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{2}$ is also a root." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296672 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297164 1069 "q-28520" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297164 1071 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $-5$, find the equation." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297164 1072 #uuid "13a60a36-8728-5ba0-bfa1-5c53c4ad86ee" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297164 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-3\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=3\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $-5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+9) & =0 \\\\ -5{x}^{2}-45 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297102 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297165 1069 "q-28556" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297165 1071 "Find ${g}^{″}(4)$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297165 1072 #uuid "88cefa50-35a4-5398-bbcf-d41d22641c1f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297165 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{5/2}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{2}){x}^{3/2}-2+0 \\\\  & =10{x}^{3/2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & ={(10{x}^{3/2}-2)}^{′} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{1/2}-0 \\\\  & =15{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(4) & =15\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =15(2) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297073 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297166 1069 "q-285703" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297166 1071 "![](images/da/dafaf2421aaa9be80c472b4dde7c941defb94f7f3533fda4ebb2fdcee2b60e31.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297166 1072 #uuid "3bb44f47-acad-5cce-bb2a-a2f32b8da827" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297166 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/cd/cd9363a3cc686ae45f49c982f4b31ad75b7c7897dcf7e8af58e530dba6946e1d.png)\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘} \\\\  & =21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈16.086\\,9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{43}^{∘} \\\\  & =28\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{43}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈19.096\\,0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & ≈16.086\\,9+19.096\\,0 \\\\  & ≈35.182\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $35.2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297037 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297167 1069 "q-285737" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297167 1071 "![](images/48/48dec69962b09e9c189de23c398db63066b6da8d62a04f1cd01fdc9f333f899f.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297167 1072 #uuid "8934630b-77d1-55ee-b8d1-51a8be5db47e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297167 1074 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/d6/d6a654d3154ba8af8fc4f7654e62c6bacfede7017322aeed21a143e2634dcd14.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)(8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈13.856,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 13.856 \\\\  & =27.712.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram is $28.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296684 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297168 1069 "q-285740" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297168 1071 "![](images/b6/b69082ef86283bc842ce487b62901e8946ffff53ab8323a6fc846f68c5869aae.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$   Then, the area of $△BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is{{field-1}}${\\text{cm}}^{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297168 1072 #uuid "1777d556-717d-5159-9602-61018070f640" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297168 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 24\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{75}^{∘} \\\\  & =11.591… \\\\  & ≈12{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest integer**.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296655 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297169 1069 "q-285754" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297169 1071 "![](images/d6/d6923f5883ea770ce243eb23594740461a689c01f63de0421bc6f5d73a39531d.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $7{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$   Then, the area of $△BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is{{field-1}}${\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297169 1072 #uuid "268ddd5a-7e79-59f1-bca0-b18bcf34aa98" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297169 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 28\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =9.899… \\\\  & ≈10{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest integer**.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =3{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296942 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297170 1069 "q-285769" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297170 1071 "![](images/33/33aff6da54b72146f89bbd580a889a4050bff89bca86ddb6b74b80f62875a470.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $5{\\text{ km}}^{2}.$   Then, the area of $△BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is{{field-1}}${\\text{km}}^{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297170 1072 #uuid "0087987a-958f-539c-9cad-7ab2dae7694f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297170 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2.5\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{63}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{63}^{∘} \\\\  & =6.682… \\\\  & ≈7{\\text{ km}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest integer**.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =7-5 \\\\  & =2{\\text{ km}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297101 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297171 1069 "q-285786" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297171 1071 "![](images/af/aff2ca3dac212832eb3b2219678dab3f4029319ce6b09f8c134f9dcc99b72640.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $13{\\text{ mi}}^{2}.$   Then, the area of $△BCD$, rounded to the **nearest integer**, is{{field-1}}${\\text{mi}}^{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297171 1072 #uuid "9ed8b4aa-f539-5288-91e4-4588aa44c54e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297171 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 6.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 32.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈16{\\text{ mi}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest integer**.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =16-13 \\\\  & =3{\\text{ mi}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296865 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297172 1069 "q-28655" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297172 1071 "Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ when $h(x)={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297172 1072 #uuid "d0b4e552-dbf9-536f-981b-a75efbb821de" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297172 1074 "First, we express $h$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nh={5}^{u},\\,u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\,v=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\frac{1}{v},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{v})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${h}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{5}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296936 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297173 1069 "q-28662" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297173 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{10}^{x-2}?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297173 1072 #uuid "576bf0cb-2319-5c69-b55f-c2fe9031a1d5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297173 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={10}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/0f/0fae83bb6962fff7c1b182e4ba2e548a95e4fbe5527e14eb6607b905129e503e.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $2$ units to the right to give $y={10}^{x-2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(2,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb9d5c110388591d1965117724fe683bb0af6e45f4e3b60c95bc959b14d76afe.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{10}^{x-2}.$ The point $(2,1)$ moves to $(2,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/5c/5cae0046253d7ebc3c93000bad49d4d0e8b3653597fcbd2f16468c679ebe0116.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296822 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297174 1069 "q-28675" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297174 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).$\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $\n- $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297174 1072 #uuid "4be11add-d875-5219-b83f-33d16bf37abb" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297174 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297056 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297175 1069 "q-2876" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297175 1071 "![](images/2b/2bd252d1bbecdb6cf3d15af44d5599f9694dbbfc339241f90ed2e49cfb279000.png)\n\nA giant inflatable ball is losing air uniformly, causing its radius to change at a rate of $\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}.$ If the radius at a certain moment is $24\\,\\text{in},$ what is the rate at which the surface area is decreasing?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297175 1072 #uuid "0190ee81-75d1-57db-a7fc-6862e592bd65" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297175 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=24\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }$ and $r=24.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the ball is decreasing at a rate of $16{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297150 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297176 1069 "q-287975" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297176 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297176 1072 #uuid "e508284d-0585-5c0f-a542-d91d66f78ddf" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297176 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p-3,\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p-3=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296902 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297177 1069 "q-288051" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297177 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+c={x}^{2}+2x+5\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $c?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297177 1072 #uuid "18b174ac-d1dd-5c5d-9174-02898287a7b5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297177 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the constant terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the constant is $c.$ On the right-hand side, the constant is $5.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nc=5.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296965 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297178 1069 "q-288066" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297178 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-3u+4=-3u-1$\n- $2u+5=2u+8$\n- $6u-2=3u+1$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297178 1072 #uuid "1b892079-10a1-5d68-a34f-01695fac566e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297178 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+4 & =-3u-1 \\\\ -3u+4+3u & =-3u-1+3u \\\\ 4 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $-3u+4=-3u-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+5 & =2u+8 \\\\ 2u+5-2u & =2u+8-2u \\\\ 5 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $2u+5=2u+8$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6u-2 & =3u+1 \\\\ 6u-2-3u+2 & =3u+1-3u+2 \\\\ 3u & =3 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $6u-2=3u+1$ has the unique solution $u=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296933 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297179 1069 "q-288070" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297179 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-4y+2=-4y-5$\n- $6y+3=6y-1$\n- $5y-7=2y+1$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297179 1072 #uuid "9e1fdbd8-4927-548c-aafb-62f0ed007aa2" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297179 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2 & =-4y-5 \\\\ -4y+2+4y & =-4y-5+4y \\\\ 2 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $-4y+2=-4y-5$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+3 & =6y-1 \\\\ 6y+3-6y & =6y-1-6y \\\\ 3 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $6y+3=6y-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-7 & =2y+1 \\\\ 5y-7-2y+7 & =2y+1-2y+7 \\\\ 3y & =8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5y-7=2y+1$ has the unique solution $y=\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296651 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297180 1069 "q-288072" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297180 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $2z-4=2z+1$\n- $5z+6=5z+6$\n- $3z+2=z+4$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297180 1072 #uuid "22403a37-d9b4-5866-bee2-a28d9c27c3b3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297180 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-4 & =2z+1 \\\\ 2z-4-2z & =2z+1-2z \\\\ -4 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $2z-4=2z+1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+6 & =5z+6 \\\\ 5z+6-5z & =5z+6-5z \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+2 & =z+4 \\\\ 3z+2-z-2 & =z+4-z-2 \\\\ 2z & =2 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3z+2=z+4$ has the unique solution $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297069 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297181 1069 "q-288073" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297181 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $3w+2=2w+2$\n- $4w-7=4w-7$\n- $6w+5=6w+9$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297181 1072 #uuid "ae9bca83-e68f-5756-9519-1447b4343c01" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297181 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w+2 & =2w+2 \\\\ 3w+2-2w-2 & =2w+2-2w-2 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3w+2=2w+2$ has the unique solution $w=0.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w-7 & =4w-7 \\\\ 4w-7-4w & =4w-7-4w \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $w.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w+5 & =6w+9 \\\\ 6w+5-6w & =6w+9-6w \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $w.$ Therefore, the equation $6w+5=6w+9$ has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296783 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297182 1069 "q-288074" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297182 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $9v-5=4v+2$\n- $2v+3=2v+7$\n- $5v-1=5v-1$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297182 1072 #uuid "6480ee2c-c900-5448-85b3-32f94538971e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297182 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9v-5 & =4v+2 \\\\ 9v-5-4v+5 & =4v+2-4v+5 \\\\ 5v & =7 \\\\ v & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $9v-5=4v+2$ has the unique solution $v=\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+3 & =2v+7 \\\\ 2v+3-2v & =2v+7-2v \\\\ 3 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $v.$ Therefore, the equation $2v+3=2v+7$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5v-1 & =5v-1 \\\\ 5v-1-5v & =5v-1-5v \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $v.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297015 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297183 1069 "q-288135" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297183 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4py=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297183 1072 #uuid "e5cf9073-af1e-5711-af30-8567dad9e870" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297183 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=4p,\\,b=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4p=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296662 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297184 1069 "q-288143" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297184 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)x=6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297184 1072 #uuid "9d84e977-5f76-5e6a-86a0-a01877993e64" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297184 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-3(q+4),\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(q+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-3(q+4)}{-3} & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\ q+4 & =0 \\\\ q & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296681 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297185 1069 "q-288144" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297185 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)z=10\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297185 1072 #uuid "1bd38fc7-d1ae-53ac-8f14-e2748bd742a7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297185 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $az=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-5(p-9),\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(p-9) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-5(p-9)}{-5} & =\\frac{0}{-5} \\\\ p-9 & =0 \\\\ p & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296990 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297186 1069 "q-288406" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297186 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{2}+mx-6=-5{x}^{2}-3x-6\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297186 1072 #uuid "7d2e83b2-fdb4-5969-995c-ff8567f8cf5b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297186 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the linear terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x$ is $m.$ On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x$ is $-3.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296802 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297187 1069 "q-288425" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297187 1071 "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $-3x+2=-3x+7$\n- $6x+1=6x+5$\n- $7x-4=6x-6$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297187 1072 #uuid "d5426119-4ea0-5c84-bb6c-cf460c622db0" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297187 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+2+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 2 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+5 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+5-6x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4 & =6x-6 \\\\ 7x-4-6x & =6x-6-6x \\\\ x-4 & =-6 \\\\ x-4+4 & =-6+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $7x-4=6x-6$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296930 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297188 1069 "q-288427" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297188 1071 "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $2x-5=x-2$\n- $6x+1=6x+9$\n- $-4x+3=-4x+3$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297188 1072 #uuid "31ef6d07-7ebc-5067-b0a5-3f361bec8d1c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297188 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296656 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297189 1069 "q-288436" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297189 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5px=10x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297189 1072 #uuid "556d6e5b-c151-55e9-9606-63f27cad6bd7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297189 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296843 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297190 1069 "q-288440" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297190 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n-4kx=8x\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297190 1072 #uuid "e80edf76-d1e3-576c-b5c7-753b9159f67b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297190 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-4k,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-4k=8.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=-2.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296706 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297191 1069 "q-288444" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297191 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+3x+b=-2{x}^{2}+3x-6\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $b?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297191 1072 #uuid "9ce8b4ce-44b9-5fd2-b920-97238e5e6b2e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297191 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the constant terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the constant is $b.$ On the right-hand side, the constant is $-6.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nb=-6.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297049 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297192 1069 "q-288446" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297192 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-7x+n=4{x}^{2}-7x+2\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $n?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297192 1072 #uuid "58ad2e63-cdf6-5c7b-81fa-f1b54115ee73" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297192 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the constant terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the constant is $n.$ On the right-hand side, the constant is $2.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nn=2.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297034 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297193 1069 "q-288448" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297193 1071 "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\nk{x}^{2}+4x+1=-2{x}^{2}+4x+1\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297193 1072 #uuid "11341405-5802-5b8a-9b1b-4eb7b33961b2" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297193 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the quadratic terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is $k.$ On the right-hand side, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is $-2.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nk=-2.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297032 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297194 1069 "q-288451" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297194 1071 "The equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+mx+c={x}^{2}+7x-5\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $m$ and $c$ are constants. What is the value of $m+c?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297194 1072 #uuid "ffcc7b45-1246-5e48-a7e0-93379ccd5076" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297194 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $m,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nm=7.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $c,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-5.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nc=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+c & =7+(-5) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296922 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297195 1069 "q-288452" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297195 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+nx+d={x}^{2}-12x-8\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $n$ and $d$ are constants. What is the value of $n+d?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297195 1072 #uuid "12cdfe66-5367-5a52-9239-ea7358c10305" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297195 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $n,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-12.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nn=-12.\n$$\n\n- The constant term on the left-hand side is $d,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-8.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nd=-8.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+d & =-12+(-8) \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297078 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297196 1069 "q-288459" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297196 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\np{x}^{2}+qx-6={x}^{2}+7x-6\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. What is the value of $pq?$\n\n$pq=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297196 1072 #uuid "21782176-d73e-5390-9d5f-27477ada9aef" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297196 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ on the left-hand side is $p,$ and on the right-hand side it is $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\np=1.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $q,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nq=7.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}pq & =1\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296879 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297197 1069 "q-288463" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297197 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\nt{x}^{2}+wx+6=4{x}^{2}-2x+6\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $t$ and $w$ are constants. What is the value of $tw?$\n\n$tw=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297197 1072 #uuid "ca7895f4-e297-534c-b2c4-cf6508e9fd34" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297197 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ on the left-hand side is $t,$ and on the right-hand side it is $4.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nt=4.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $w,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nw=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tw & =4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296792 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297198 1069 "q-288464" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297198 1071 "The equation \n\n$$\nu{x}^{2}+vx-3=-7{x}^{2}+x-3\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $u$ and $v$ are constants. What is the value of $uv?$\n\n$uv=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297198 1072 #uuid "22653ce0-62f6-5a50-92fd-d48ea787012d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297198 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ on the left-hand side is $u,$ and on the right-hand side it is $-7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nu=-7.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $v,$ and on the right-hand side it is $1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nv=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}uv & =(-7)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296698 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297199 1069 "q-288468" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297199 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\nt(2{x}^{2}-3x+1)=-6{x}^{2}+9x-3\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $t$ is a constant. What is the value of $t?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297199 1072 #uuid "58c900de-ded0-51c6-94b4-0ca8530168c9" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297199 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means that after distributing $t$ on the left-hand side, the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nt(2{x}^{2}-3x+1)=2t{x}^{2}-3tx+t\n$$\n\nThis must equal the right-hand side. So, we have\n\n$$\n2t{x}^{2}-3tx+t=-6{x}^{2}+9x-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms. \n\nComparing the ${x}^{2}$ terms, we have\n\n$$\n2t=-6\\,⇒\\,t=-3.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $t=-3$ produces the correct linear coefficient and constant term:\n\n- $-3t=(-3)(-3)=9\\,✓$\n\n- $t=-3\\,✓$\n\nSo, our solution is valid." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297022 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297200 1069 "q-288583" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297200 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297200 1072 #uuid "73256cc5-b18c-5ffc-bb1b-9018e84f6eb6" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297200 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=3$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=3+(-3)=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296817 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297201 1069 "q-288584" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297201 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297201 1072 #uuid "c1e37e75-7d0c-5923-8a40-5f706dc21da1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297201 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+3=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=2.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=2$ and $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=2+3=5.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296985 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297202 1069 "q-288591" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297202 1071 "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $3x+2=4x+1$\n- $2x-5=3x-3$\n- $5x-4=5x-4$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297202 1072 #uuid "43eccfac-bd80-5f7b-abff-3250eee5dc70" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297202 1074 "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297000 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297203 1069 "q-288596" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297203 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q+9)y=-6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297203 1072 #uuid "1328ee8c-fdcc-59c5-a877-6291af207d84" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297203 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q+9,\\,b=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq+9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q+9 & =0 \\\\ q & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296652 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297204 1069 "q-288622" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297204 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297204 1072 #uuid "afc322c5-285c-5560-a114-d1da26bf18af" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297204 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+2=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n5n=15.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=5$ and $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=5+3=8.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296994 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297205 1069 "q-288623" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297205 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297205 1072 #uuid "48147a3d-8103-52a6-b2f5-5907d423b5d5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297205 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2k=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{9}{2}$ and $r=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=\\frac{9}{2}+6=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296992 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297206 1069 "q-288627" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297206 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p+q?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297206 1072 #uuid "9f55693d-3dd0-5310-a538-5dcb7f69af97" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297206 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $p$ and $q$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nq=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $p=8$ and $q=20,$ we have\n\n$$\np+q=8+20=28.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296718 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297207 1069 "q-288634" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297207 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n3(p+2)x+7=10\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297207 1072 #uuid "d326f3de-7c8d-596a-800c-00170de0cabb" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297207 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2)x+7 & =10 \\\\ 3(p+2)x+7-7 & =10-7 \\\\ {{3(p+2)}_{⏟}}_{a}x & ={{3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3(p+2)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3(p+2)}{3} & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\ p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296790 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297208 1069 "q-288644" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297208 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n(k-2)v-3=5\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297208 1072 #uuid "e9e21b0c-1002-52c2-8ff0-0958a8c28640" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297208 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-2)v-3 & =5 \\\\ (k-2)v-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ {{(k-2)}_{⏟}}_{a}v & ={{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nk-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2 & =0 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296774 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297209 1069 "q-288646" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297209 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n(m+6)x+2=7\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297209 1072 #uuid "219bc978-7d21-5b4f-a212-8daca1973e88" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297209 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+6)x+2 & =7 \\\\ (m+6)x+2-2 & =7-2 \\\\ {{(m+6)}_{⏟}}_{a}x & ={{5}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nm+6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+6 & =0 \\\\ m & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296661 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297210 1069 "q-288648" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297210 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q-5)u+9=0\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297210 1072 #uuid "4f6400a6-9f87-55b0-bcbe-d4a53fe5731a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297210 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q-5)u+9 & =0 \\\\ (q-5)u+9-9 & =0-9 \\\\ {{(q-5)}_{⏟}}_{a}u & ={{-9}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-5 & =0 \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296687 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297211 1069 "q-288662" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297211 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n3py=5y+8\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297211 1072 #uuid "6cf4ece6-fb8b-5a6f-963d-f97832f4331b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297211 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ay=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3py & =5y+8 \\\\ 3py-5y & =5y+8-5y \\\\ 3py-5y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $y$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3p-5)y=8.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ay=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(3p-5)}_{⏟}}_{a}y={{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-5 & =0 \\\\ 3p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 3p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296729 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297212 1069 "q-288673" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297212 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n3qz+4=7z+1\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297212 1072 #uuid "fa4e94a9-eff0-5fbb-b2d3-1dcbd9a3e5ee" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297212 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $7z$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz+4 & =7z+1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =7z+1-7z \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz-7z+4 & =1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4-4 & =1-4 \\\\ 3qz-7z & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3q-7)z=-3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(3q-7)}_{⏟}}_{a}z={{-3}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3q-7=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3q-7 & =0 \\\\ 3q-7+7 & =0+7 \\\\ 3q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{3q}{3} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296803 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297213 1069 "q-288674" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297213 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n5ru+3=2u+6\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297213 1072 #uuid "9c5e8ea4-ee94-5a1f-a3c1-cf0a3b495462" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297213 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $2u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru+3 & =2u+6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =2u+6-2u \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru-2u+3 & =6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3-3 & =6-3 \\\\ 5ru-2u & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(5r-2)u=3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(5r-2)}_{⏟}}_{a}u={{3}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5r-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5r-2 & =0 \\\\ 5r-2+2 & =0+2 \\\\ 5r & =2 \\\\ \\frac{5r}{5} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297154 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297214 1069 "q-288675" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297214 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n-4mw+5=-3w+1\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297214 1072 #uuid "169f6fd4-529f-5b4e-9fcd-e754c540805a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297214 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $3w$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+5 & =-3w+1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =-3w+1+3w \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+3w+5 & =1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ -4mw+3w & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-4m+3)w=-4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(-4m+3)}_{⏟}}_{a}w={{-4}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-4m+3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4m+3 & =0 \\\\ -4m+3-3 & =0-3 \\\\ -4m & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{-4m}{-4} & =\\frac{-3}{-4} \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296650 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297215 1069 "q-288678" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297215 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3pz-8=-z-6\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297215 1072 #uuid "93ece185-5396-5f04-8ab8-086b4b609861" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297215 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $z$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz-8 & =-z-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-z-6+z \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz+z-8 & =-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8+8 & =-6+8 \\\\ -3pz+z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-3p+1)z=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(-3p+1)}_{⏟}}_{a}z={{2}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3p+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3p+1 & =0 \\\\ -3p+1-1 & =0-1 \\\\ -3p & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{-3p}{-3} & =\\frac{-1}{-3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296658 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297216 1069 "q-288697" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297216 1071 "The equation \n\n$$\n(cx-8)(x+d)=4{x}^{2}-20x+24\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $c$ and $d$ are constants.What is the value of $c+d?$\n\n$c+d=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297216 1072 #uuid "a64cb6f6-9175-5f68-b76b-3cdbf16e7815" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297216 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the product on the left-hand side must expand to match the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(cx-8)(x+d) & =c{x}^{2}+cdx-8x-8d \\\\  & =c{x}^{2}+(cd-8)x-8d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we equate the expanded form to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nc{x}^{2}+(cd-8)x-8d=4{x}^{2}-20x+24.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms.\n\n- Equating the coefficients of ${x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nc=4.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n-8d=24\\,⇒\\,d=-3.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that values $c=4$ and $d=-3$ produce the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cd-8 & =4\\cdot (-3)-8 \\\\  & =-12-8 \\\\  & =-20\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the right-hand side. So, our solution is valid.\n\nFinally, we compute the value of $c+d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c+d & =4+(-3) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296772 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297217 1069 "q-288698" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297217 1071 "The equation\n\n$$\n(mx+10)(x+n)=-5{x}^{2}+20x-20\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $m$ and $n$ are constants. What is the value of $m+n?$\n\n$m+n=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297217 1072 #uuid "a4f5ef23-a449-5593-bd96-099b1a0f4869" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297217 1074 "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the product on the left-hand side must expand to match the right-hand side.\n\nFirst, let's expand the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(mx+10)(x+n) & =m{x}^{2}+mnx+10x+10n \\\\  & =m{x}^{2}+(mn+10)x+10n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we equate the expanded form to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nm{x}^{2}+(mn+10)x+10n=-5{x}^{2}+20x-20.\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the coefficients of corresponding terms.\n\n- Equating the coefficients of ${x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-5.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n10n=-20\\,⇒\\,n=-2.\n$$\n\nLet's verify that values $m=-5$ and $n=-2$ produce the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}mn+10 & =(-5)(-2)+10 \\\\  & =10+10 \\\\  & =20\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the right-hand side. So, our solution is valid.\n\nFinally, we compute the value of $m+n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+n & =-5+(-2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296979 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297218 1069 "q-288779" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297218 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+8x-1=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297218 1072 #uuid "9327d307-f99f-5c05-bdb3-72ba3bd6cdca" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297218 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=3,\\,b=8,\\,c=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297076 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297219 1069 "q-288816" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297219 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-17x-30=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297219 1072 #uuid "f379d5ac-6fe2-5ce8-b120-ae7f8d1580fe" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297219 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=6,\\,b=-17,\\,c=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-30)}{6}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296927 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297220 1069 "q-288817" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297220 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+24x+15=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297220 1072 #uuid "1e8803d7-bfe3-5bbf-a5a9-c44bfab57d17" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297220 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=5,\\,b=24,\\,c=15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{15}{5}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297112 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297221 1069 "q-288821" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297221 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+427x-58\\,971=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297221 1072 #uuid "beafac37-bf7d-5666-8a69-22a49ade8191" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297221 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=427,\\,c=-58\\,971.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-58\\,971)}{1}=-58\\,971.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296721 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297222 1069 "q-288825" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297222 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+41x-391=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297222 1072 #uuid "edeac1f1-6a23-50a2-b07f-f8b62b91f0ee" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297222 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=41,\\,c=-391.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-391)}{1}=-391.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296682 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297223 1069 "q-288828" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297223 1071 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $4.$ The sum of the solutions equals $-9,$ and the product of the solutions equals $10.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}  \n$=0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297223 1072 #uuid "9c5ff3ca-7b32-5b21-985e-22e95fcba93d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297223 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=4.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $-9.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ -9 & =-\\frac{b}{4} \\\\ 36 & =b \\\\ b & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $10.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{c}{4} \\\\ 40 & =c \\\\ c & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=4,\\,b=36,\\,c=40\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+36x+40=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296989 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297224 1069 "q-288829" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297224 1071 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $5.$ The sum of the solutions equals $8,$ and the product of the solutions equals $-12.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}  \n$=0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297224 1072 #uuid "c6cfabaa-d2cd-5776-a8f3-c4a8a73efdd3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297224 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $5.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=5.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $8.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ 8 & =-\\frac{b}{5} \\\\ -40 & =b \\\\ b & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-12.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -12 & =\\frac{c}{5} \\\\ -60 & =c \\\\ c & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=5,\\,b=-40,\\,c=-60\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-40x-60=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296780 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297225 1069 "q-288830" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297225 1071 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $6.$ The sum of the solutions equals $-5,$ and the product of the solutions equals $-7.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}  \n$=0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297225 1072 #uuid "399b5396-e7ec-5525-888f-7055d7b7163a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297225 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $6.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=6.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $-5.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ -5 & =-\\frac{b}{6} \\\\ 30 & =b \\\\ b & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $-7.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ -7 & =\\frac{c}{6} \\\\ -42 & =c \\\\ c & =-42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=6,\\,b=30,\\,c=-42\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+30x-42=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297114 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297226 1069 "q-288851" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297226 1071 "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1.$ The sum of the solutions equals $-8,$ and the product of the solutions equals $20.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297226 1072 #uuid "a6859666-1b52-57d7-8d91-13ed1eecb8b0" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297226 1074 "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=1.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $-8.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ -8 & =-\\frac{b}{1} \\\\ 8 & =b \\\\ b & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $20.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ 20 & =\\frac{c}{1} \\\\ c & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=8,\\,c=20\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+8x+20=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296739 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297227 1069 "q-28902" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297227 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=3$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297227 1072 #uuid "b48b10b3-0d9d-5485-bda2-3198c7563d19" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297227 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/31/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296810 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297228 1069 "q-28981" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297228 1071 "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.08)^{x}?$\n\n- Its range is $y>0$\n- It is an increasing function\n- $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297228 1072 #uuid "7c7808ae-1f8f-5217-b026-8ee08973156b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297228 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(0.08)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b4e3b5119c92d02bbceb786fefd238182c7c84ca0f4abc85a19f2df86a6ba267.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(0.08)}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement II is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296717 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297229 1069 "q-28993" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297229 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297229 1072 #uuid "afa93ab3-2677-50cc-b4ef-12db62bfbb63" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297229 1074 "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={45}^{∘},{315}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=45\\cdot 315=14\\,175.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296649 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297230 1069 "q-29005" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297230 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297230 1072 #uuid "fd623a0f-67ba-5c4f-8eff-33385ede435e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297230 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296907 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297231 1069 "q-29046" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297231 1071 "What is the solution to $\\,-3<5-2x\\le 1?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297231 1072 #uuid "7b57eafc-c371-518b-a9bf-5395b44481a4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297231 1074 "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual. \n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & <5-2x\\le 1 \\\\ -3-5 & <5-2x-5\\le 1-5 \\\\ -8 & <-2x\\le -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-2.$ Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & <-2x\\le -4 \\\\ \\frac{-8}{-2} & >x\\ge \\frac{-4}{-2} \\\\ 4 & >x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $4>x\\ge 2$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: $2\\le x<4.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296993 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297232 1069 "q-29055" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297232 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\,-2<\\,2x-2\\,<2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297232 1072 #uuid "8c9076ec-2d2e-5447-b094-af3daa134994" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297232 1074 "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & <\\,2x-2\\,<2 \\\\ -2+2 & <2x-2+2<2+2 \\\\ 0 & <2x<4 \\\\ \\frac{0}{2} & <\\frac{2x}{2}<\\frac{4}{2} \\\\ 0 & <x<2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $0<x<2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297075 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297233 1069 "q-29186" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297233 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297233 1072 #uuid "f64999ff-874c-549e-b51f-2404ee85f7ed" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297233 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x={120}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot 240=28\\,800.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296747 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297234 1069 "q-29207" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297234 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297234 1072 #uuid "2e82d568-df50-578f-b07c-119397489f57" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297234 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296708 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297235 1069 "q-29222" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297235 1071 "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297235 1072 #uuid "1b7b2409-b453-5c01-9897-3a973253713f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297235 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296805 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297236 1069 "q-29233" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297236 1071 "![](images/e2/e20c8b73d3e46aa90c5d37a30d8343c98d6c6b0f3f17a232580583a8ae812125.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x})+c.$ Find the value of $k$ and $c.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297236 1072 #uuid "f23367e0-950a-5c59-9df0-bbc47cc55447" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297236 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k({2}^{x})+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ so $c=-2.$\n\nTherefore, our function takes the form\n\n$$\ny=k({2}^{x})-2.\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k({2}^{x})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k({2}^{0})-2 \\\\ 1 & =k(1)-2 \\\\ 1 & =k-2 \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=3$ and $c=-2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296823 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297237 1069 "q-29247" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297237 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=20-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297237 1072 #uuid "bb45da11-83cf-50bf-9d9f-98689c5db9f8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297237 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{1}{5})=(11.309\\,9…)^{∘}≈{11.310}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${11.310}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={11.310}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{11.310}^{∘}={191.310}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n11.310\\cdot 191.310≈2\\,164\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296760 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297238 1069 "q-29349" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297238 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297238 1072 #uuid "493ac61e-d5aa-548d-a691-2dfa99e52431" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297238 1074 "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n2x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSolving this for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296987 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297239 1069 "q-29378" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297239 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-6?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297239 1072 #uuid "a890f17e-1965-548f-8730-d240f1dabda9" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297239 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/7b/7b9f7e5ca81a615cb16ccb7a69f9d1669366944d0615041ed8e960f59b8e24dd.png)\n\n- Reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/00/00e5e14399b9fd259327e1c8fbbccd43018df8a4c78b7ed7d957819c81eef7f2.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units down to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-6.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(-1,-7)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=-6.$\n\n![](images/c9/c99dc12a5a34672fa5b5bd180d777c1640b657d0725d115b9f6decfab6296b18.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297082 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297240 1069 "q-29380" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297240 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the function $y=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x+4}?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297240 1072 #uuid "8a8cfa2c-147b-5173-8f78-6a88b030a1e0" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297240 1074 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/40/401c0b3fea9c98884cf8678bc33ff6442a66f3e766ba89c26917a172b21cfbac.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $4$ units to the left to give $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x+4}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-4,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1487a47526f4c9f546edb8911ed174bdebe80952ee226b0d59f7ac78a122c18.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x+4}.$ The point $(-4,1)$ moves to $(-4,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/52/520a39616c45b8c7fbe389057015d47b7f2270960b426614606596043451ce8d.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296875 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297241 1069 "q-29393" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297241 1071 "Which of the diagrams shows the solution of the following system?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y<2 \\\\ y\\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297241 1072 #uuid "68096a4e-e6f1-53a9-8c36-d84f96df3bed" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297241 1074 "To graph the first inequality $y<2,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=2.$\n\n- Since we have a strict inequality, the line itself is *not* included in the solution, so the line is dashed.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line, so we shade *below* the line.\n\nTo graph the second inequality $y\\ge -2,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=-2.$\n\n- Since we have a \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *above* the line.\n\nThe intersection of the solutions is the following:\n\n![](images/e9/e949882745de4727e3f611b8f958b64ed6fc08c0097508970501a16f68374cf2.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297006 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297242 1069 "q-29438" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297242 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297242 1072 #uuid "97f191d4-6a4f-5a4f-8505-bbd4c324e609" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297242 1074 "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296887 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297243 1069 "q-29556" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297243 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297243 1072 #uuid "670b4725-4f5f-5e5b-81ee-7bebf6dbdf85" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297243 1074 "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297012 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297244 1069 "q-29608" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297244 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$\n- $tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297244 1072 #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297244 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297131 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297245 1069 "q-29673" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297245 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297245 1072 #uuid "380886ef-0fbf-54bc-9579-e4e21dd4f7ae" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297245 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{1})^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4-\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296924 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297246 1069 "q-29674" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297246 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297246 1072 #uuid "2d4f351a-f228-5a07-9853-4d4d86412f5a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297246 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{-\\frac{4}{3}+1}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-3(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{1}}) \\\\  & =3(1-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{3}}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{3}} \\\\  & =3-\\sqrt[3]{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296982 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297247 1069 "q-29692" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297247 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+22$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297247 1072 #uuid "44692078-9fa6-5b9b-a4ac-0bb1d6759de1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297247 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2 & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+22 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})=0.244\\,978≈0.245\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $0.245\\,0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.245\\,0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi +0.245\\,0=-2.896\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=0.245\\,0,-2.896\\,6.$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n0.245\\,0\\cdot (-2.896\\,6)≈-0.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297105 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297248 1069 "q-29734" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297248 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-9, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}-5, & x\\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297248 1072 #uuid "7ee241ee-6c9b-581f-8e3e-00ecaf1e5967" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297248 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}(x-9)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{10}(\\frac{x}{5}-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-9x)|}_{0}^{5}+{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{10}-5x)|}_{5}^{10} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(5)^{2}}{2}-9(5)-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2}+9(0)]+[\\frac{(10)^{2}}{10}-5(10)-\\frac{(5)^{2}}{10}+5(5)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{25}{2}-45]+[10-50-\\frac{25}{10}+25] \\\\  & =-50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297121 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297249 1069 "q-29739" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297249 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297249 1072 #uuid "8f0fe807-1fba-5baa-b6b7-6741269e860e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297249 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6-5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1}+{(6x-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}]+(6(2)-\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-6(1)+\\frac{5(1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}]+[12-10-6+\\frac{5}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296853 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297250 1069 "q-29742" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297250 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-9, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-1, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297250 1072 #uuid "598039da-8879-5b3c-ab71-e359f8b00731" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297250 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x-9)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(\\frac{x}{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}-9x)|}_{0}^{3}+{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}-x)|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{3(3)^{2}}{2}-9(3)-\\frac{3(0)^{2}}{2}+9(0)]+[\\frac{(6)^{2}}{6}-6-\\frac{(3)^{2}}{6}+3] \\\\  & =[\\frac{27}{2}-27]+[6-6-\\frac{3}{2}+3] \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296787 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297251 1069 "q-29746" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297251 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297251 1072 #uuid "85c7a44a-100b-573e-a54f-0ac01a135d81" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297251 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297152 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297252 1069 "q-29747" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297252 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297252 1072 #uuid "078e36f8-ac04-5dc6-872a-8aad0a6d9513" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297252 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{(-3)}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296812 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297253 1069 "q-29780" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297253 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{4}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297253 1072 #uuid "953eaea1-7562-55f0-8e7b-723ba8d5e0ae" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297253 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{4}(2{x}^{3/2}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of three integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2{x}^{3/2}+3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4}{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{4}+x\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4}+x\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}({4}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2})+\\frac{3}{2}({4}^{2}-{0}^{2})+(4-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 32+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 16+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}+28 \\\\  & =\\frac{268}{5}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297086 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297254 1069 "q-29831" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297254 1071 "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297254 1072 #uuid "f3f7435c-4593-5eb7-979c-b6475dc0ac4f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297254 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-3$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297042 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297255 1069 "q-29834" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297255 1071 "What is the antiderivative of $-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297255 1072 #uuid "eec65b66-7ead-5d6e-b179-5aacd453b9d8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297255 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296743 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297256 1069 "q-29838" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297256 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297256 1072 #uuid "ac24bc33-26ae-5498-b55b-b6545ea0f7e9" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297256 1074 "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297081 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297257 1069 "q-29885" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297257 1071 "Calculate $\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297257 1072 #uuid "a9a35970-b3a0-5bae-b863-30b3731d8d68" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297257 1074 "First, we apply the sum rule and get\n\n$$\n\\int ({x}^{2/3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, using the power rule in the first term, and taking out the coefficient in the second term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\sqrt{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3+1}}{\\frac{2}{3}+1}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296807 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297258 1069 "q-29886" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297258 1071 "Find the integral of $2{x}^{7/3}-\\frac{1}{3x}$ with respect to $x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297258 1072 #uuid "8c693357-ab6d-5b31-ab83-a3f2da57f6b4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297258 1074 "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{7/3}-\\frac{1}{3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{7/3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7/3+1}}{(\\frac{7}{3}+1)}-\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{10/3}-\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297003 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297259 1069 "q-29888" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297259 1071 "Find the integral of $\\frac{3}{{x}^{-1}}+3{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297259 1072 #uuid "a09c1ef5-54ac-5fb8-be82-1b666b5326c2" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297259 1074 "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{3}{{x}^{-1}}+3{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296849 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297260 1069 "q-29915" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297260 1071 "Find the integral of $\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297260 1072 #uuid "77e80b12-44f5-5ee6-b586-d6d7898a0c32" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297260 1074 "Simplifying the exponents and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2-3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296666 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297261 1069 "q-29953" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297261 1071 "Given that $\\frac{14{p}^{3}}{3{q}^{3}}=a$ and  $\\frac{7{p}^{3}}{15{q}^{3}}=b,$ what is the value of $a÷b?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297261 1072 #uuid "6857c1e3-b4e6-5a35-bde2-f265ec9c08c1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297261 1074 "To compute\n\n$$\na÷b=\\frac{14{p}^{3}}{3{q}^{3}}÷\\frac{7{p}^{3}}{15{q}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14{p}^{3}}{3{q}^{3}}÷\\frac{7{p}^{3}}{15{q}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{14{p}^{3}}{3{q}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{15{q}^{3}}{7{p}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 7{p}^{3}}{3{q}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot 3{q}^{3}}{7{p}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{7{p}^{3}}}{\\cancel{3{q}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{3{q}^{3}}}{\\cancel{7{p}^{3}}} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & = \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296686 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297262 1069 "q-29970" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297262 1071 "If $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ then what is $Y?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297262 1072 #uuid "964f86e1-4f13-55f4-a1ab-8caf3da2db2d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297262 1074 "Since $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 & = \\\\ \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296713 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297263 1069 "q-29979" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297263 1071 "![](images/35/355d3770234a7b0c9a65965d65ad3807adedc7e9c6c8a23ddbe25bb34a0dd5d0.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and the line $y=-2.$ Which of the following generates the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297263 1072 #uuid "732288fe-6fc0-55c4-91c2-b913a4ca0e96" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297263 1074 "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.$ \n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arccos}(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution for the given equation is \n\n$$\nx={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296912 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297264 1069 "q-30048" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297264 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}-20x-21=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297264 1072 #uuid "b44bf3d4-0727-5c97-b85d-0008ae48eb05" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297264 1074 "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x & =21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 10\\cdot x & =21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 10\\cdot x+{10}^{2}-{10}^{2} & =21 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 10\\cdot x+{10}^{2})-100 & =21 \\\\ (x-10{)}^{2}-100 & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-10{)}^{2}-100 & =21 \\\\ (x-10{)}^{2} & =121\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-10,q=121,$ and $p+q=111.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-10{)}^{2} & =121 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-10{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{121} \\\\ x-10 & =\\pm 11 \\\\ x & =10\\pm 11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10+11=21 \\\\ x & =10-11=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=21.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297107 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297265 1069 "q-30057" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297265 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}-4x+4=1$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p-q,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297265 1072 #uuid "9036c737-fb24-58d0-8b5a-f6c590da27dc" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297265 1074 "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 2+{2}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-2,q=1,$ and $p-q=-3.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2+1=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2-1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ Thus, the sum is $4.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296951 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297266 1069 "q-300762" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297266 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13{x}^{13})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297266 1072 #uuid "d8095d2b-4a15-5ff5-8d3a-358ba3c8e137" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297266 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }13{x}^{13} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13\\cdot {x}^{13}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{13}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $13$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13{x}^{13})=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296955 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297267 1069 "q-300764" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297267 1071 "If $p(x)=-3{x}^{5}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{7}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)\\cdot q(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297267 1072 #uuid "b7a2c77c-6afe-560a-891d-7d57e249dc14" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297267 1074 "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-3{x}^{5})=-3{x}^{7}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{4}{{x}^{7}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297125 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297268 1069 "q-300766" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297268 1071 "If $p(x)=10{x}^{8}$ and $q(x)=2{x}^{8},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297268 1072 #uuid "2609e259-0172-5823-9ba6-a03f9513fe52" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297268 1074 "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10{x}^{8})}{(2{x}^{8})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296983 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297269 1069 "q-30080" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297269 1071 "Two cars, $A$ and $B,$ are both initially at the point $O,$ and they both depart from $O$ at the same time. Car $A$ travels due east, and its distance from $O$ is given by $3t\\,\\text{ft}.$ Car $B$ travels due north, and its distance from $O$ is $2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{ft}.$ Here, $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the distance between the two cars after $2$ seconds?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297269 1072 #uuid "ff7f5106-2da2-5fdb-b8a4-ffffc41203d1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297269 1074 "Let's draw a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/aa/aa3f39f65b0785cfca2d1545e4bf331281f523f5e19777372dbb44096bdb4223.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car $A$ and point $O$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car $B$ and point $O$, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between car $A$ and car $B.$\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the distance traveled by the cars after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=3(2)=6\\text{ ft}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=y(2)=2(2)^{2}=8\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =10\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe velocity of the cars is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=3$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(t)=4t.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocities are\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(2)=8\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=3\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(3)+2(8)(8) & =2(10)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 164 & =20\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is changing at a rate of  $8.2\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296660 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297270 1069 "q-301603" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297270 1071 "![](images/2f/2fd351e75519ab242ba99028214cbdd35c61cea6acdf69a39eb30395e2f7eaaa.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297270 1072 #uuid "000c53a8-54ac-54e2-82d8-62a98a2afdd1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297270 1074 "Applying the product rule and the root rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[5]{f(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-32.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{-32} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-2$ causes $y=-2{x}^{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{6})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296862 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297271 1069 "q-301609" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297271 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297271 1072 #uuid "4fbc60ab-10b2-5a55-952e-88966a85c0bd" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297271 1074 "First note that $ln⁡(0.9)<0.$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{4}$ looks like an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $ln⁡(0.9)$ causes $y=ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{4})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297155 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297272 1069 "q-301610" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297272 1071 "If $p(x)=-20{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297272 1072 #uuid "c991e854-9c79-53ca-925f-63f0269438ee" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297272 1074 "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-20{x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }((-20\\cdot \\frac{1}{5})\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-4$ causes $y=-4{x}^{2}$ to be a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296914 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297273 1069 "q-302056" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297273 1071 "If $p(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=9{x}^{6},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297273 1072 #uuid "8d93923b-2491-5385-9e01-fb8b3e2e0923" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297273 1074 "First, let's calculate ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{4}\\cdot (-5{x}^{2})=-5{x}^{6}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}}{(9{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{5}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296731 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297040 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297274 1069 "q-302096" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297274 1071 "Find the left-sided and the right-sided limits at $x=2$ for the function\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ \\frac{3-\\sqrt{x+7}}{{x}^{2}-1} & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297274 1072 #uuid "635155a9-ded0-5a46-b382-83b094ba82e1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297274 1074 "Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $g(x)={x}^{2}+x-2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+2-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x\\ge 2,$ so we use the expression $g(x)=\\frac{3-\\sqrt{x+7}}{{x}^{2}-1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{x+7}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{2+7}}{{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{9}}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=4,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296670 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297089 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297275 1069 "q-302142" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297275 1071 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}a{x}^{2}-3x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-3\\le x<1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-bx\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=4.$ Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297275 1072 #uuid "a7a0760e-ea55-55bc-901b-79315b1aad3c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297275 1074 "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to be equal to $4,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $4.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $-3\\le x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=a{x}^{2}-3x+2.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & =a({1}^{2})-3(1)+2 \\\\  & =a-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left-sided limit must be equal to $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =4 \\\\ a-1 & =4 \\\\ a & =4+1 \\\\ a & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $1\\le x\\le 3,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-bx.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}-bx) \\\\  & =3({1}^{2})-b(1) \\\\  & =3-b\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-sided limit must be equal to $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =4 \\\\ 3-b & =4 \\\\ b & =3-4 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296850 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297276 1069 "q-302235" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297276 1071 "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{8{x}^{6}+5}{2{x}^{5}-7x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297276 1072 #uuid "58972e97-803a-5a72-ad54-417f9bf3a2a1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297276 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{8{x}^{6}+5}{2{x}^{5}-7x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(8x+\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})}{(2-\\frac{7}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x+0}{2-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,4x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296680 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297277 1069 "q-302240" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297277 1071 "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x<0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297277 1072 #uuid "a9813cef-279a-5fd4-8e50-a7601280fe38" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297277 1074 "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|8x-7|=-(8x-7).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|8x-7|}{-5x+2}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8x-7)}{-5x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{7}{x})}{(\\frac{-5x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(8-\\frac{7}{x})}{(-5+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(8-0)}{-5+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{8}{5}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297094 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297278 1069 "q-302242" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297278 1071 "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}}$ is $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $c.$\n\n$c=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297278 1072 #uuid "f75bce11-338e-5172-9aa9-a70e3ac0a182" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297278 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{18{x}^{4}+5x}{c{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{18{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(18+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(c-\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+0}{c-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{c} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 36 & =3c \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296689 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297279 1069 "q-302299" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297279 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+7}-2}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297279 1072 #uuid "1d6fb489-c02a-55f5-8269-014a0828765f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297279 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+7}-2}{x+3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{-3+7}-2}{-3+3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+7}-2}{x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+7}-2)(\\sqrt{x+7}+2)}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+7}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+7})^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+7}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{(x+7)-4}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+7}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{x+3}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+7}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\cancel{x+3}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(\\sqrt{x+7}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+7}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-3+7}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296925 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297280 1069 "q-302322" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297280 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297280 1072 #uuid "9aeff8cd-3746-55c8-a1bf-e9ff6439faf8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297280 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{0+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{(x+1)-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{x}(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\sqrt{x+1}+1) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+1}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296749 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297281 1069 "q-302331" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297281 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297281 1072 #uuid "4deb9bbb-6d66-5551-9151-59c868082f63" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297281 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3})}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =6(2\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296971 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297282 1069 "q-302435" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297282 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297282 1072 #uuid "135bab25-0c8b-562b-9627-75046e3fd02e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297282 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot {5}^{x})=2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/02/02d46e13c84fc575f489a9870f3b1213eb3d180f3f5ef6e6a546d3acfe4b2282.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound  as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=2\\cdot \\infty =\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296827 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297283 1069 "q-302650" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297283 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297283 1072 #uuid "081bea40-3640-5a40-9984-5a664f4f2206" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297283 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}{\\pi x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)}{\\pi \\cdot (1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296738 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297284 1069 "q-302689" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297284 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297284 1072 #uuid "58f94bd2-6052-5c0f-852e-d8d0ce5e7319" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297284 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2):$\n\n![](images/a7/a7e36a5c534a63199ea97985f1301db0c7675adc8fa0d17f33735c61eec9e871.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297011 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297285 1069 "q-30269" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297285 1071 "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297285 1072 #uuid "91d38446-45fe-5f55-b13c-726fa96fa8c7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297285 1074 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296644 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297286 1069 "q-302690" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297286 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297286 1072 #uuid "b1394c62-62a1-53db-8987-e127821ad30e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297286 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/fc/fc06a4e63f38a315dd3944f65f871cca2dbdddee26ec268e857f79e5f4ab8848.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297005 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297287 1069 "q-30271" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297287 1071 "![](images/b0/b088ed9136d2b81e48752659369b24d2296924702a843629cca27d063c62b4df.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+x+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297287 1072 #uuid "014873d6-1fe1-5356-8070-03bad625ff4d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297287 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+x+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-0.5$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+x+2$ between the lower limit $x=-0.5$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-0.5}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296766 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297288 1069 "q-302820" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297288 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297288 1072 #uuid "959dac60-c266-5e5e-bc27-a754d438b32c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297288 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{10x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{2x}{x}+\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(2+3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot [{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2+3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (2+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296947 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297289 1069 "q-302827" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297289 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297289 1072 #uuid "34a796fc-8b16-58cc-88b1-3c76c0415be6" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297289 1074 "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\theta } \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296858 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296863 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296864 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296984 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1069 "q-302857" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1071 "The largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}+2x-15}$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}},\n{{field-3}}$)⋃($\n{{field-4}}$,\\infty ).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1072 #uuid "05b6d94f-c5aa-516f-87d3-61775b08a5eb" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297290 1074 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}+2x-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+5}{{x}^{2}+5x-3x-15} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+5}{(x+5)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne -5.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=-5$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,3),$ and $(3,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (-5,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296820 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296892 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297291 1069 "q-302871" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297291 1071 "The largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-8))$ is continuous is\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297291 1072 #uuid "6b53e7ab-cf73-5078-854f-f46c3802fafe" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297291 1074 "The function is a composition of two functions, a logarithm function, and a cosine function.\n\nThe cosine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm function.\n\nLikewise, the logarithm function $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-8)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we set the expression under the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-8>0\\,⟹\\,x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(4,\\infty )$, and the function is continuous on this interval." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296869 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297292 1069 "q-30368" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297292 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297292 1072 #uuid "60fb4a8e-0b44-516d-9ebb-a8d91494c67e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297292 1074 "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296786 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297293 1069 "q-3038" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297293 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297293 1072 #uuid "7ade3ef7-2470-5279-802e-68d9ba79206f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297293 1074 "Let $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296872 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297294 1069 "q-30382" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297294 1071 "![](images/e8/e8a9dfb3ff189e96089c73ead8a3b44f04aa1684894c44203afb8884ad75c9e1.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297294 1072 #uuid "faf2d8c2-4461-517c-a4d3-4a8680913bb9" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297294 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-1.5$ and $x=0.5.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2$ between the lower limit $x=-1.5$ and the upper limit $x=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1.5}^{0.5}(-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297060 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297295 1069 "q-304035" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297295 1071 "Given that $f(x)=6{x}^{7}+2{e}^{x}$, the exact value of the slope of the tangent line at $x=-1$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297295 1072 #uuid "0f66d07e-3ca1-595a-b1d7-b4c775329676" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297295 1074 "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{7}+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =6(7{x}^{6})+2{e}^{x} \\\\  & =42{x}^{6}+2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(42{x}^{6}+2{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =42(-1)^{6}+2{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =42\\cdot 1+\\frac{2}{e} \\\\  & =42+\\frac{2}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296667 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297296 1069 "q-304038" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297296 1071 "Given that $f(x)=3\\cdot {8}^{x}+12x+10$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297296 1072 #uuid "4df22161-f9b7-5077-b623-7a70a19c609f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297296 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x}+12x+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\cdot {8}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({8}^{x})+12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =3(ln⁡(8){8}^{x})+12(1)+0 \\\\  & =3ln⁡(8){8}^{x}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296938 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297297 1069 "q-304068" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297297 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(-1,-1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297297 1072 #uuid "251ee9eb-ffd1-5d74-9058-3b8ed977eb92" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297297 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3x+2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-2}-4{x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{3}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{4}{(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =-3+4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=1$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,-1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =1\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y+1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296828 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296839 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297298 1069 "q-304090" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297298 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=4x-{x}^{2}$ at the point  $(1,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297298 1072 #uuid "39619091-ee32-5930-b214-e50ce700ac23" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297298 1074 "Let $f(x)=4x-{x}^{2}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=4-2(1)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =2(x-1) \\\\ y & =2x-2+3 \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)+1 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,1).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296883 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297299 1069 "q-304130" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297299 1071 "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=4x-2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(4,12).$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297299 1072 #uuid "8d2746f0-484f-5af7-8356-aa63ec30ff54" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297299 1074 "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =4-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,12)$ is\n\n$$\nm={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297054 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297300 1069 "q-30468" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297300 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4)=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297300 1072 #uuid "58af19f4-fcf8-555c-b281-db48de8422e8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297300 1074 "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-4) & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-16 & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{16})=86.416\\,678{…}^{∘}≈{86.417}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${86.417}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={86.417}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/15/15e99d348206191535a2b6fb9d85893e666abd16f5f9fc61fdf4e19424e2fde9.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{86.417}^{∘} \\\\  & ={273.583}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=86.417\\cdot 273.583≈23\\,642,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296688 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297301 1069 "q-30578" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297301 1071 "$-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297301 1072 #uuid "dd3c17c9-23ec-5ace-941a-93cdddad613f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297301 1074 "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296648 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297302 1069 "q-30588" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297302 1071 "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297302 1072 #uuid "90ca11f6-8893-5beb-98a2-24781da24a2d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297302 1074 "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297083 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297303 1069 "q-30618" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297303 1071 "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t$\nfor $x>-1.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297303 1072 #uuid "1b5efccc-d381-506e-babf-3ae77a11a1c4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297303 1074 "The function ${t}^{2}-2t+5$ is continuous on $[-1,x]$ where $x>-1.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t & ={x}^{2}-2x+5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296826 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297304 1069 "q-30627" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297304 1071 "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297304 1072 #uuid "be6bd7f3-2e23-5861-b95e-2eb6ae25ed99" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297304 1074 "The function $a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1$ is continuous on $[-5,x],$ where $x>-5.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}t & =a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296954 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297305 1069 "q-3068" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297305 1071 "Solve the inequality $-1<\\frac{6x-8}{2}<1.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297305 1072 #uuid "5071ba6e-1ca6-52b8-be49-8361dcae5f8e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297305 1074 "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we multiply by $2$ to eliminate the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <\\frac{6x-8}{2}<1 \\\\ -1\\cdot 2 & <\\frac{6x-8}{2}\\cdot 2<1\\cdot 2 \\\\ -2 & <6x-8<2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue isolating $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & <6x-8<2 \\\\ -2+8 & <6x-8+8<2+8 \\\\ 6 & <6x<10 \\\\ \\frac{6}{6} & <\\frac{6x}{6}<\\frac{10}{6} \\\\ 1 & <x<\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $1<x<\\frac{5}{3}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297098 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297306 1069 "q-30771" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297306 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ on the interval $[-2,1].$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297306 1072 #uuid "f95328a8-4512-5fae-94f0-1be783b35629" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297306 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-{x}^{2})|}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4}-{1}^{2})-(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{4}-(-2)^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{3}{4}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296733 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297307 1069 "q-30776" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297307 1071 "Find the average value of the function $g(x)=2{x}^{3}-x-1$ on the interval $[-2,0].$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297307 1072 #uuid "72cc8fee-c912-578c-b6ad-a221076e3800" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297307 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2{x}^{3}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x)|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{2}-(-2))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-(8-2+2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-8) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296897 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297308 1069 "q-30827" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297308 1071 "Solve the inequality $3{x}^{2}+x>x-2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297308 1072 #uuid "9e7b67ef-dc8c-54b7-aae2-ef9f928ec760" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297308 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+x & >x-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x-x & >x-2-x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Inequality**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $3{x}^{2}>-2$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y=3{x}^{2}+2.$ :\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $3{x}^{2}+2$   is positive, the parabola opens upward.  The desired inequality $(y>0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/39/39e923bcf71c650441cbe39bd173056a008e5926679271109df479ca76bd8294.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297062 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297309 1069 "q-30851" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297309 1071 "Solve $5{x}^{2}-16>9.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297309 1072 #uuid "46f1a747-782e-56a6-9147-e76e2d37f5f8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297309 1074 "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-16 & >9 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & >9+16 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & >25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |x| & >\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{5})\\cup (\\sqrt{5},+\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>5$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-5>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-5\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\sqrt{5})(x+\\sqrt{5}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nhence $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-5$   is positive, the parabola  opens upward.  Therefore, the solution set to the  given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots (but does not include them):\n\n![](images/52/52b970419b3dbb9498e6b7b05d031195b1e8fae9bdba0428dd72f84f0101095b.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{5})\\cup (\\sqrt{5},\\infty ).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296844 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297310 1069 "q-30872" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297310 1071 "Find the numbers $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=-6{x}^{2}+14x-5$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297310 1072 #uuid "16b1bc24-13e7-5da4-a569-33e3816b732d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297310 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(-6{x}^{2}+14x-5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & ={\\frac{1}{c}(-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-5x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(-2{c}^{3}+7{c}^{2}-5c)-0] \\\\ 0 & =-2{c}^{2}+7c-5 \\\\ 2{c}^{2}-7c+5 & =0 \\\\ (c-1)(2c-5) & =0 \\\\ c & =1,\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296975 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297311 1069 "q-30873" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297311 1071 "For the curve $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297311 1072 #uuid "5fda2bca-a959-55aa-9acd-07aede112347" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297311 1074 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8.5$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5-8.5=-7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({10}^{0})-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5-8.5 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-7).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296710 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297137 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297312 1069 "q-308823" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297312 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+1$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297312 1072 #uuid "ac03aacc-0b7e-5a7f-8554-ee3b669a879c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297312 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{(1)^{2}}=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297157 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297313 1069 "q-308849" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297313 1071 "Given that $y=8ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297313 1072 #uuid "a09e33e6-12c2-544c-8fa4-02ef6f3be0de" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297313 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8ln⁡x) \\\\  & =8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297108 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297314 1069 "q-308854" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297314 1071 "Given the function  $y=3{x}^{e}+ln⁡x,$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297314 1072 #uuid "395be2fc-ff05-5c2f-8d61-c5ab0d5aca86" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297314 1074 "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{e}+ln⁡x) \\\\  & =3e{x}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(3e{x}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =3e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =3e+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296705 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297315 1069 "q-308866" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297315 1071 "Given the function  $y=2{log}_{3}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{e}^{x},$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297315 1072 #uuid "8688ee43-a87d-5757-aeae-1697946d83af" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297315 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{log}_{3}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{1}+5{e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4+5e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296719 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297316 1069 "q-30893" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297316 1071 "Within the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ determine the average value of $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297316 1072 #uuid "87e893b1-43cc-5695-982d-b173b104605b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297316 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})))] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(0+(-1))-(0+1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296880 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297317 1069 "q-30903" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297317 1071 "A biologist found that the population $P$ of some bacteria in a petri dish can be modeled by the function $P(t)=200+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}t),$ where $t$ is the number of hours after $12$ noon. Find the average number of bacteria during the period from $12$ noon to $4$ p.m." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297317 1072 #uuid "c3d43bb3-dee9-539e-921d-9e390abc8b05" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297317 1074 "Since the function is valid starting from $12$ P.M., we can conclude our interval is $[0,4].$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}P(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-0}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(200+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}t))\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}(200t-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\,t}{4}))|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(200\\cdot 4-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 4}{4}))-(200\\cdot 0-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 0}{4}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(800-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(800-\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1))+\\frac{32}{\\pi }\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[800+\\frac{32}{\\pi }+\\frac{32}{\\pi }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(800+\\frac{64}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =200+\\frac{16}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297151 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297318 1069 "q-30922" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297318 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297318 1072 #uuid "38f9635e-72c4-5968-9945-fe2ceda9691e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297318 1074 "If we let $u=-2x,$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-5{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-5{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-5{\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{e}^{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{-2})}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}({4}^{-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{1}{16}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{15}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{75}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296919 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297319 1069 "q-30953" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297319 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}}\\frac{1}{5}x{e}^{2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297319 1072 #uuid "d27a3338-cb0f-5ca8-99dd-ea66c994efb5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297319 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u=2{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}}\\frac{1}{5}x{e}^{2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}}{e}^{2{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(25-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297095 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297320 1069 "q-30973" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297320 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297320 1072 #uuid "34a4e0e2-73c5-54dc-8277-4607c07e4179" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297320 1074 "If we let $u=6x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of  $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{3}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{3}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{{3}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}){|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(729-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{728}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{364}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297149 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297321 1069 "q-30978" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297321 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}x{4}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297321 1072 #uuid "7481f370-fa2c-5189-a8ea-31c09e902fb8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297321 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the new limits of integration:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{4}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{4}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{4}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296769 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297322 1069 "q-30991" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297322 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=6({x}^{2}-cos⁡x)+5.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297322 1072 #uuid "8745f56e-112e-51d0-a4cd-8f6c067107b5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297322 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6({x}^{2}-cos⁡x)+5) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2x+6sin⁡x+0 \\\\  & =12x+6sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296799 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297323 1069 "q-310122" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297323 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{{x}^{5}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297323 1072 #uuid "b3820859-5781-5537-93c5-21af16442024" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297323 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫{x}^{5}$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{{x}^{5}}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296714 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297324 1069 "q-310144" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297324 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(5{x}^{2}+4)$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297324 1072 #uuid "f22eb9bd-4b64-597a-920f-3c1311c73e8e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297324 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=5{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & ={\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(1)}{5({1}^{2})+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296831 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297325 1069 "q-310145" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297325 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={log}_{4}⁡(6x-2)+7$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297325 1072 #uuid "d9148bcf-019e-5ab0-a8b9-eb415bd0a6d1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297325 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}⁡(u)+7,\\,u=6x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}{|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3(1)-1)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297148 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297326 1069 "q-310149" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297326 1071 "Given that $y=log⁡({x}^{5}-2),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297326 1072 #uuid "c7331908-3e29-5baf-bb7e-ba78549a5c63" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297326 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=log⁡(u),\\,u={x}^{5}-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)ln⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296778 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297327 1069 "q-310171" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297327 1071 "Find ${g}^{(4)}(x)$ for $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$\n\n${g}^{(4)}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297327 1072 #uuid "ca192f94-8d93-544f-8a75-568decc353d8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297327 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the fourth derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-6{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296764 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297328 1069 "q-310179" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297328 1071 "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3{x}^{4}-5)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297328 1072 #uuid "a87f80fe-d2d2-50e5-bf92-8b685bd67b58" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297328 1074 "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,u=3{x}^{4}-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={sec}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot 12{x}^{3} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}⁡(3{x}^{4}-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296997 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297329 1069 "q-310180" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297329 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297329 1072 #uuid "4c450049-c1ac-524d-8d79-0a2c2ad034fe" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297329 1074 "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is \n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296770 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297330 1069 "q-310222" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297330 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2{x}^{3}),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297330 1072 #uuid "7a2ac9db-9f05-59f3-8c0a-71e8b95b390c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297330 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot 6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{csc}^{2}⁡(2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297058 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297331 1069 "q-3103" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297331 1071 "If ${5}^{2a-5}=\\frac{1}{{25}^{a+1}}$ then $a=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297331 1072 #uuid "6ea2dd05-d2d3-5da1-bad5-e07ed434ba91" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297331 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $25$ as ${5}^{2},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{25}^{a+1}} \\\\ {5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{({5}^{2})}^{a+1}} \\\\ {5}^{2a-5} & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{2(a+1)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2a-5} & ={5}^{-2(a+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-5 & =-2(a+1) \\\\ 2a-5 & =-2a-2 \\\\ 4a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297132 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297332 1069 "q-31122" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297332 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{16})}^{x}+4.5?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297332 1072 #uuid "c134dba9-db81-5965-88cc-1e938dfc4f3b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297332 1074 "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{16})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/7a/7a630b4582049bf2a2144ce484069a5301e7363a35da38e7d86ff2fee22543d4.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4.5$ units up. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=4.5,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,5.5).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{16})}^{x}+4.5$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35c5c0ef4850371b35fa43b9f2944e98919199bbacf41453973167522cf04f35.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297053 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297333 1069 "q-311277" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297333 1071 "The values of functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=5$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $6$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})$ at $x=5.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=5}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297333 1072 #uuid "1e00a045-7deb-531f-a9c3-4bec570ef120" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297333 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)-f(x){g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=5$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)})|}_{x=5} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(5)g(5)-f(5){g}^{′}(5)}{(g(5))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)(-2)-(3)(6)}{(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297020 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297334 1069 "q-31153" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297334 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)$ at the point where $x=2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297334 1072 #uuid "6d3355be-a5a0-56e5-8db1-b9c9b33d002a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297334 1074 "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is given by ${f}^{′}(2).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 2+5) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297104 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297335 1069 "q-31163" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297335 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297335 1072 #uuid "a15a3bf2-ccf2-5108-b6fe-372cf79c1d17" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297335 1074 "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=11-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296745 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297336 1069 "q-31221" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297336 1071 "![](images/52/5287302ea411868ac0d2f8d06e5487dabcb421f6e462e0af0db30e34c328b8d4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={g}^{″}(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297336 1072 #uuid "d0f92157-ca87-5a01-ba7d-435db9df653b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297336 1074 "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,5)$ only. Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(0,5)$ only." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297045 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297337 1069 "q-31224" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297337 1071 "![](images/e7/e7f211223ade0c30c2ba94b1fd4c63d2d74fd6064032d799cdbe56cc0302300c.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297337 1072 #uuid "44a492fb-9c88-5afa-8a5d-d4acf94834f3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297337 1074 "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-3.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297146 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297338 1069 "q-31225" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297338 1071 "![](images/e8/e8f5f537ad63035cac39ea672d4fd2bfff61d97590f79f83277e6654c9c05893.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={h}^{″}(x),$ where the function $h(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $h(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297338 1072 #uuid "b4032471-f652-52ec-b101-8fb77484b374" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297338 1074 "A function $h(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${h}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={h}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${h}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5).$ Therefore, the function $h(x)$ is concave up on the intervals $(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296874 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297339 1069 "q-312258" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297339 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y=6x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297339 1072 #uuid "9c578341-fd5b-5ea9-b094-029b8faa3d2a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297339 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ [3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}]-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ (3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4})}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297156 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297340 1069 "q-31228" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297340 1071 "![](images/30/305fa76994ff0be3c43894226eaa82d3d9e016c6add11cd48d80486c50643ed0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297340 1072 #uuid "e2faeba0-b737-514d-8d25-1eceee0628d8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297340 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-4,-2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2)$ only." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296972 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297341 1069 "q-31234" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297341 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297341 1072 #uuid "1709b5ea-f5ce-5d07-be0b-066860df686f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297341 1074 "We begin with the graph of ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/16/16a1a985e5c0e15bf9d21cd70eaa63887bfa39dd81ef01720af2d1ea5d2b1516.png)\n\nNext, add $10$ to ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ to give ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10$, a vertical shift by $10$ units up. Point $(0,1)$ on ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}$ becomes $(0,11)$ on ${(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}+10.$ The horizontal asymptote becomes $y=10.$ The new graph is shown below.   \n\n \n\n![](images/af/af910cd3774ce1df9455df13bb1cd94354129b1127c921abb04013540889b41a.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297079 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297342 1069 "q-312358" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297342 1071 "If $f(x)=\\text{arccot }x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1),$ calculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297342 1072 #uuid "75fdefb6-9025-5f7e-8537-d9b67757e76d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297342 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $\\text{arccot}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arccot }x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\text{arccot }x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296671 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297343 1069 "q-312364" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297343 1071 "Calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=5arcsec({x}^{2}+3)$ at $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297343 1072 #uuid "99fbb8d5-fc1d-5b4d-b999-a3255c91ff23" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297343 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $arcsec$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsec\\,x)=\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5arcsec({x}^{2}+3)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{|{x}^{2}+3|\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{|{x}^{2}+3|\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{10x}{|{x}^{2}+3|\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{10(2)}{|{2}^{2}+3|\\sqrt{({2}^{2}+3)^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{|7|\\sqrt{49-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7\\sqrt{48}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{7\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296788 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296928 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297344 1069 "q-312391" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297344 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{arcsec\\,x}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\frac{1}{arcsec\\,x}\\,-$\n\n{{field-2}} \n$\\frac{1}{|x|{arcsec}^{2}\\,x}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297344 1072 #uuid "a21be179-3e67-5f75-b560-ed7e4e4d7e25" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297344 1074 "Here, we let $u(x)={x}^{2}+5$ and $v(x)=arcsec\\,x,$ and apply the quotient rule.\n\nWe use the formulae\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2x\\,\\text{and}\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x)\\cdot arcsec\\,x-({x}^{2}+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}}{(arcsec\\,x)^{2}}. \\\\  & =2x\\,\\frac{1}{arcsec\\,x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}+5}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\frac{1}{|x|{arcsec}^{2}\\,x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296944 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297345 1069 "q-312451" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297345 1071 "Given that $f(x)={e}^{4{x}^{5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297345 1072 #uuid "487ee789-7ad7-529c-b59f-58c0bcaef3bf" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297345 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (20{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}{e}^{4{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296773 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297346 1069 "q-312462" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297346 1071 "Given that $f(x)={5}^{{x}^{3}+1},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,{5}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297346 1072 #uuid "96fff31f-332c-5572-b655-bad437de2f44" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297346 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={5}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{u} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296678 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297347 1069 "q-31249" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297347 1071 "$\\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297347 1072 #uuid "a909a720-f9c7-52e5-a8a4-abd3492e6b5f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297347 1074 "First, we factor out the constant $3.$ Then, we integrate the exponential function and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296732 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297348 1069 "q-31252" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297348 1071 "$\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297348 1072 #uuid "42f6b48b-f5fd-5245-b823-ea76a025e943" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297348 1074 "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296856 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297349 1069 "q-31253" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297349 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297349 1072 #uuid "2244c901-0342-58ec-9ffa-72ac8cad54d3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297349 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x+1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+1}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297109 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297350 1069 "q-31254" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297350 1071 "$\\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297350 1072 #uuid "73bc3d8b-49e8-5e01-8096-212f791e5c3c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297350 1074 "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-2{e}^{x}+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296762 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297351 1069 "q-312549" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297351 1071 "Given that $x={y}^{2}-y,$ express $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297351 1072 #uuid "33ddb022-5204-5f5d-a7fe-9687a12ba27b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297351 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2y-1}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297136 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297352 1069 "q-312553" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297352 1071 "Given a curve $x=3y-{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(0,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297352 1072 #uuid "01978fc9-f56f-57cf-8868-52118ee62fe2" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297352 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3y-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =3(1)-2y \\\\  & =3-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3-2y}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(0,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(0,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296735 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297353 1069 "q-31256" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297353 1071 "$\\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297353 1072 #uuid "8203576d-9480-5aa7-b8ae-908a8c1708f4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297353 1074 "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296741 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297354 1069 "q-31257" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297354 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297354 1072 #uuid "076a6ee5-4c7d-5342-8736-e5be0b0283bd" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297354 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}+4x+3]^{′}}{[2x+3]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297130 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297355 1069 "q-312578" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297355 1071 "![](images/e0/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297355 1072 #uuid "19309cb7-ace3-5af0-908b-5924987e950f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297355 1074 "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296929 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297356 1069 "q-312579" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297356 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and the point $P(2,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297356 1072 #uuid "8004f487-3cf9-55ce-a70e-fc271afc0b4e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297356 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296673 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297357 1069 "q-312580" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297357 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+{2}^{x}$ and the point $P(0,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297357 1072 #uuid "3d10818b-b3a5-537c-a4e3-55ca3cdd0aba" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297357 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+{2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x+{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296711 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297358 1069 "q-312581" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297358 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x-2}{x+2}$ and the point $P(-1,-5)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-5,-1)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297358 1072 #uuid "cc4f3f66-846e-5e4b-a228-d406681446ad" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297358 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1,-5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x-2}{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1,-5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5,-1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296903 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297359 1069 "q-312583" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297359 1071 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297359 1072 #uuid "1edb97ff-e669-542c-ba07-6fedf01189b1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297359 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297041 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297360 1069 "q-312584" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297360 1071 "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-3$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297360 1072 #uuid "1f15cb00-451b-5523-bf6f-8460aecf67b4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297360 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=5,$ $g(2)=4,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5+\\frac{4}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =-15+\\frac{4}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-15-4 \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297120 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297361 1069 "q-312585" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297361 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(8)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297361 1072 #uuid "189fb590-9ad6-5a31-897c-faebe34b5258" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297361 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-4x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4({2}^{3})-4(2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296981 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297362 1069 "q-312588" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297362 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={4}^{x}+2.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297362 1072 #uuid "c5b33d7a-7226-5bcd-82ec-47a327f13d58" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297362 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(6),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(6).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(6)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(6) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x}+2 & =6 \\\\ {4}^{x} & =4 \\\\ {4}^{x} & ={4}^{1} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(6)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={4}^{x}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & ={4}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297023 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297363 1069 "q-312589" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297363 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297363 1072 #uuid "51bfbb7b-301f-58c9-a0d4-6df0eb0a9569" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297363 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=3.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2)+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296814 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297364 1069 "q-31260" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297364 1071 "$\\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297364 1072 #uuid "9412645b-c765-5ef4-bf32-1eae5303c300" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297364 1074 "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296945 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297365 1069 "q-31271" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297365 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8}{x-2}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297365 1072 #uuid "76ee6af4-046e-58d7-b011-2d83573fbafd" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297365 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8]^{′}}{[x-2]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{4{x}^{3}-4x}{1} \\\\  & =4(2)^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296871 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297366 1069 "q-312780" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297366 1071 "![](images/b2/b269001ff5a3a1a1d86125157ee2f5b77d89ebcf0041f8d403dbc931b796c5e8.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297366 1072 #uuid "2b5b23fa-be02-551f-968d-c9191c07be80" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297366 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(5,7).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$ and $y=7$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 49 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{49}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{49}{5}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{49}{5}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{49}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{49}{10y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296665 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297367 1069 "q-31320" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297367 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297367 1072 #uuid "b56b53a5-1b6c-546f-ab86-f9c5279b7a79" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297367 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sec}⁡u,\\,u=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-1)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296782 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297368 1069 "q-313343" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297368 1071 "![](images/20/204afb23fc438b047c72d6233b59b70c4741ee16a30a57c084682a3d14fd2188.png)\n\nA thin soap film is stretching over time. The graph of its surface area $A(t)$, in square centimeters, after $t$ minutes is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the surface area at $t=2$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nAfter $2$ minutes, the surface area increases at a rate of {{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297368 1072 #uuid "44c27b76-302d-5154-8c65-42a79c8677d1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297368 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{A(t+h)-A(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=2$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{A(2+2)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{A(4)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-10}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $2$ minutes, the surface area changes at a rate of approximately $15\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296986 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297369 1069 "q-313352" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297369 1071 "The ambient temperature in a laboratory varies over time. After $t$ seconds, the temperature (in degrees Celsius) is modeled by\n\n$$\nΘ(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+5)+18.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the temperature as a function of $t?$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change is\n{{field-1}} degrees Celsius per second." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297369 1072 #uuid "e63de395-d6f3-59df-b981-4296f11ee6d3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297369 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the temperature is ${Θ}^{′}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Θ}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+5)+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2t+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the ambient temperature is $\\frac{2}{2t+5}$ degrees Celsius per second." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296761 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297370 1069 "q-313361" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297370 1071 "In an acoustics lab, the ambient noise power (in decibels) at frequency $f$ (in $\\text{kHz}$) is modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(f)={2}^{-2{f}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the exact value of the instantaneous rate at which the noise power is changing with respect to frequency when $f=2\\,\\text{kHz}?$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when $f=2\\,\\text{kHz}$ is \n{{field-1}} decibels per $\\text{kHz}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297370 1072 #uuid "c83c02d7-ac76-5ed2-b203-257e722c4410" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297370 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change at which the ambient noise power is changing when $f=2$ kHz is equal to ${N}^{′}(2).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $N(f)$ with respect to $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(f) & =\\frac{d}{df}({2}^{-2{f}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={2}^{-2{f}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (-4f) \\\\  & =-4f\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{-2{f}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $f=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(2) & =-4(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{-2(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\,{2}^{-8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{256} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the ambient noise power is changing when $f=2\\,\\text{kHz}$ is $-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{32}$ decibels per $\\text{kHz}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297067 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297371 1069 "q-313364" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297371 1071 "The volume of a right square pyramid is increasing. Given that its height $h$ is always twice the length of a base, find the rate of change of the volume of the pyramid with respect to its base length $s.$\n\n*Hint*: The volume of a right square pyramid is $V=\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h.$ \n\nThe rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to $s$ is \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297371 1072 #uuid "90c4f5cf-db31-54d5-9bff-47ee6cd28950" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297371 1074 "We are given that $h=2s,$ so we can express the volume of the pyramid $V$ as a function of the base edge length $s$ by substituting $h=2s$ in the formula of the volume. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{s}^{2}(2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{d}{ds}\\,(\\frac{2}{3}{s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{d}{ds}\\,({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the rate of change of the pyramid’s volume with respect to its base length $s$ is $2{s}^{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296834 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297372 1069 "q-313365" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297372 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{x+3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${(x+3)}^{2}$ |" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297372 1072 #uuid "07a6d700-2f06-5564-a6ab-09de601041cc" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297372 1074 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+3.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)\\cdot (x+3)-(x+1)^{2}\\cdot 1}{(x+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)[2(x+3)-(x+1)]}{{(x+3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)[2x+6-x-1]}{{(x+3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x+5)}{{(x+3)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296768 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297373 1069 "q-313371" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297373 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{2x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $x{e}^{2x}$ |" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297373 1072 #uuid "a8e96d3e-9fca-5093-8b9b-5bb4483b8048" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297373 1074 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the denominator $v(x)={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})({e}^{2x})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})}{{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}(1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x{({e}^{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296854 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297374 1069 "q-31338" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297374 1071 "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297374 1072 #uuid "8d5db746-ab9b-5b5a-bc25-31e21fc4632d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297374 1074 "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $3.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296889 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297375 1069 "q-31340" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297375 1071 "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297375 1072 #uuid "ab907b07-4062-5dae-90ac-fc279f3c0e29" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297375 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{csc}⁡u,\\,u=5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=-\\pi /2} & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x){|}_{x=-\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296694 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297376 1069 "q-31352" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297376 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ when $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297376 1072 #uuid "8c9a1630-1e07-5782-a0c4-5e97e3d68c4f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297376 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u={v}^{2},\\,v=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=2v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297039 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297377 1069 "q-31361" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297377 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(5x-1).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297377 1072 #uuid "60550674-e8b4-5f20-9b57-5b71c82e6894" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297377 1074 "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=5x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5(0)-1) \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-20(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297061 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297378 1069 "q-31363" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297378 1071 "For the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297378 1072 #uuid "0865b352-f43e-56b9-8d78-8c3ca9422e6b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297378 1074 "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297028 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297379 1069 "q-313637" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297379 1071 "Given that $r=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297379 1072 #uuid "fd13c00d-5dc7-5ccb-a6d3-56bdaaf16d42" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297379 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296809 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297380 1069 "q-31365" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297380 1071 "${x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297380 1072 #uuid "32ce0355-2f90-5b5b-8476-9ad2d6f5d257" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297380 1074 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{y}^{2}={({x}^{2})}^{2}-(2y)^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({x}^{2})}^{2}-(2y)^{2}=({x}^{2}+2y)({x}^{2}-2y)\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296793 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297381 1069 "q-313723" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297381 1071 "Find ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ if $g(x)=csc⁡x-2{x}^{3}+cos⁡x.$\n\n${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297381 1072 #uuid "1bab49d0-a1b6-58fc-8a02-9d9fd89dd0f7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297381 1074 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-csc⁡x-2{x}^{3}+cos⁡x) \\\\  & =(-csc⁡xcot⁡x)-2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & =-csc⁡xcot⁡x-6{x}^{2}-sin⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-csc\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})cot\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-6{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1-6\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296744 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297382 1069 "q-313855" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297382 1071 "Given that $y=-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5x$ for $x=x(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ at the point where $x=2,$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297382 1072 #uuid "0986003f-9723-5218-93b9-2acb26bd1eed" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297382 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}+8x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $x=2,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3)\\cdot (-3\\cdot (2)^{2}+8\\cdot (2)-5)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297140 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297383 1069 "q-313938" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297383 1071 "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ that lies in the second quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=-5,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297383 1072 #uuid "f973c2da-4afc-57bc-bbf9-262e7639f470" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297383 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{9}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot (\\frac{2(-5)}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{9}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-5$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 25-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the second quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2(4)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{120}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296976 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297384 1069 "q-313939" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297384 1071 "Consider the section of the parabola ${x}^{2}-4y=5$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ on the parabola at the point where $y=11,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297384 1072 #uuid "e5c29631-c25c-5cff-aa5e-35ad9223b6b1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297384 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8$ and $y=11,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-8\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=11$ into the equation of the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=11,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(11) & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-44 & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =49 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=7,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0 \\\\ 2(7)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-32 & =0 \\\\ 14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{32}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297074 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297385 1069 "q-31394" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297385 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297385 1072 #uuid "b0501bb6-8d0d-5290-9b3d-4f26ccd12ac3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297385 1074 "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{5}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296750 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296819 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297386 1069 "q-313957" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297386 1071 "Let $B=[\\begin{aligned}x-4 & 1 \\\\ 7 & x+2\\end{aligned}]$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297386 1072 #uuid "e8e1210d-36f7-5324-a441-3fe5324d9d56" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297386 1074 "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(x-4)\\cdot (x+2)-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x-8-7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-15 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296818 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297387 1069 "q-31397" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297387 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297387 1072 #uuid "46a1f16d-d0cd-5aab-85fe-3d2691d325ef" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297387 1074 "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{3}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{3}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296683 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297388 1069 "q-31398" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297388 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297388 1072 #uuid "7cf9e06a-68e3-56b4-9085-33ef355832a1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297388 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296702 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297389 1069 "q-313982" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297389 1071 "A $15\\text{ ft}$ painter’s plank leans against a wall. The bottom end slides horizontally away from the wall at a constant rate of $3\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the top end sliding down the wall at the instant when it is $9\\text{ ft}$ above the ground? Assume the rate at which the top slides downward is **positive**.\n\nThe top end is sliding down at a speed of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297389 1072 #uuid "5d666d32-7588-5747-8ef5-70ec937f2613" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297389 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/af/afdb53c3134f18add3b07400805c76a36f3f7a6b8265a3f32e9e0dfaf1dd36e9.png)\n\nIn the diagram, \n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the plank and the wall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the plank and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the plank.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y=9\\,\\text{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- The length of the plank remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(9)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{72}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the top end of the plank is sliding down at a speed of $4\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296779 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297390 1069 "q-31399" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297390 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297390 1072 #uuid "a480eb68-e1fa-542d-9387-098493118819" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297390 1074 "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296736 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297391 1069 "q-31400" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297391 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297391 1072 #uuid "baa80704-3dfa-5ff9-bf20-e404177dc892" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297391 1074 "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{log}_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297048 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297392 1069 "q-31402" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297392 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297392 1072 #uuid "f906b7ab-49ff-57e2-8ffb-323870b5ae05" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297392 1074 "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡(3x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296833 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297393 1069 "q-31409" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297393 1071 "A brick is dropped vertically downward from a height of $176.4\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the brick to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297393 1072 #uuid "17892270-e5f5-5867-ad35-8d019ad02cf7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297393 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the brick is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The brick covers the distance of $s=176.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{176.4}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =36 \\\\ t & =\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the brick hits the ground after $t=6$ seconds." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296824 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297394 1069 "q-31411" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297394 1071 "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $88.2\\,\\text{m}$ with a velocity of  $141.12\\,\\text{km/h}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297394 1072 #uuid "6fb8fe4f-c238-57e3-90cf-0d422c1eb27a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297394 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{60\\,\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{60\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{{60}^{2}\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =39.2\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=88.2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}88.2 & =(39.2)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 18(4.9) & =8(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 18\\cancel{(4.9)} & =8\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8t-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-18)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+72}}{2} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-8\\pm 11.6619}{2} \\\\  & ≈-9.83,\\,1.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $1.83\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296775 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297395 1069 "q-31412" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297395 1071 "$\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297395 1072 #uuid "91e61cac-e373-57ad-92b8-00ce9bccf78c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297395 1074 "If we let $u=-x$, then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {5}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {5}^{u}\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296692 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297396 1069 "q-31413" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297396 1071 "$\\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297396 1072 #uuid "747ae585-9faa-50cc-acc3-b1e5b1896ba8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297396 1074 "If we let $u=5x$, then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {4}^{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {4}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{5x}}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297097 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297397 1069 "q-314139" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297397 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297397 1072 #uuid "e7f7f4a0-a2c5-5970-aa06-c71ea5fc319a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297397 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{[{x}^{3}-27]^{′}}{[x-3]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296857 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297398 1069 "q-314141" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297398 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{7{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}-9}{10-3x-7{x}^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297398 1072 #uuid "43b01262-f2cd-5db4-aeca-dee4349e9cd2" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297398 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{7{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}-9}{10-3x-7{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[7{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}-9]^{′}}{[10-3x-7{x}^{3}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{28{x}^{3}+4x}{-3-21{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{28(1)^{3}+4(1)}{-3-21(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{28+4}{-3-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{(-24)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297115 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297399 1069 "q-314152" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297399 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297399 1072 #uuid "ad42f537-44a3-537f-a759-f5a89512da15" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297399 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(4+4x){e}^{2x}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+0){e}^{0}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+6}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296679 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297400 1069 "q-314154" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297400 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297400 1072 #uuid "765690fa-b939-5bc9-8765-3caee547f880" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297400 1074 "If we plug in $x=8$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-16x+64]}^{′}}{[18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{[2x-16]}^{′}}{{[9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2}{-\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}{lim}_{x\\to 8}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}(8+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296873 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297401 1069 "q-31416" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297401 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297401 1072 #uuid "fbe47114-407d-5b49-92a6-a4e3739775b5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297401 1074 "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/ca/ca12bd6db97d982fd2a9e73dcd9d5da0248ce1a40e582787c3f73480f338a570.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53d2977f07e132aa0e3c3608e8fef82f2d058fb1a99aa828ae8b317b8de6844a.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{3}{2}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/6a/6adc189f0d16cf60044553c13d79297c04f4fbfb2806a9bde6971b203822f17a.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296696 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297402 1069 "q-31417" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297402 1071 "$\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297402 1072 #uuid "4a2ea8a4-727a-5496-81cd-8ee505de6239" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297402 1074 "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {6}^{x/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {6}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {6}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\frac{{6}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{x/3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296813 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297403 1069 "q-31419" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297403 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int x{4}^{{x}^{2}-7}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297403 1072 #uuid "e54cfe97-3d02-5434-8c76-88bdc2adef2f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297403 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u={x}^{2}-7.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{4}^{{x}^{2}-7}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {4}^{{x}^{2}-7}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {4}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{4}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{{x}^{2}-7}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296861 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297404 1069 "q-314194" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297404 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+7}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297404 1072 #uuid "2300bd8f-e3d1-544c-8ad1-127cb2f0bedc" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297404 1074 "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+7.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297118 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297405 1069 "q-314246" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297405 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{5x+1}{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297405 1072 #uuid "17dd452c-1a10-54e1-9250-6e49f35c45ce" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297405 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{5x+1}{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[5x+1]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{5}{(2x+\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297123 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297406 1069 "q-31426" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297406 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int x{5}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297406 1072 #uuid "bfc6c174-e76b-5577-8587-521bdbb4edc5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297406 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{5}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {5}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {5}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{2}/3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297057 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297407 1069 "q-31435" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297407 1071 "![](images/01/016286e82482af83783f0eef6361591861b564643d4d9f010f2b82999fcaad27.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297407 1072 #uuid "7b52cd54-7f7c-57a1-892c-73badca0d5f6" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297407 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$ Hence, $c=-4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}-4 & =-2 \\\\ k-4 & =-2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-(-4)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296659 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297408 1069 "q-3146" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297408 1071 "![](images/c6/c61d9140e5b8e57c8f75b532c6e26e22faa073e6631984a8f6a7b6f4280882e3.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region enclosed between $y={x}^{2}+4x$ and $y=x,$ shown above.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297408 1072 #uuid "96033acd-67cd-543b-8482-d4f4d5a08b94" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297408 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ x(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/6b/6b2232d5a2853b22c731c317b365822e371f3d419bfcb3e528a07f211a372553.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+4x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(x)-({x}^{2}+4x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-3)^{2}] \\\\  & =(0)-(9-\\frac{27}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296868 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297409 1069 "q-314703" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297409 1071 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6x+4$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297409 1072 #uuid "4301cf58-fa58-5a33-b220-e17f611f9369" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297409 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-6.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3(-2)^{2}-6=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-6=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3(2)^{2}-6=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $(\\sqrt{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296751 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297410 1069 "q-314706" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297410 1071 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297410 1072 #uuid "b910fdd8-b412-5151-a376-459c39a5c8ee" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297410 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2(x-1){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)(x-1+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & ={e}^{-2}(-3)(-1)=3{e}^{-2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}(-1)(1)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & ={e}^{2}(1)(3)=3{e}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296961 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297411 1069 "q-314729" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297411 1071 "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297411 1072 #uuid "b98af1ab-39e6-5660-824e-6d7ceb2ec4b8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297411 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296789 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297412 1069 "q-314735" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297412 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x-4)+2.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297412 1072 #uuid "449da400-6327-594c-aa38-cfddd89692d8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297412 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x-4)+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(5x-4)}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x-4)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(5x-4)}^{2}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+{(5x-4)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297160 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297413 1069 "q-314738" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297413 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297413 1072 #uuid "78ab970d-f949-563c-b0af-4172efd56054" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297413 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(({x}^{2}+5)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}))-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+5))}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(({x}^{2}+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+(2{x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3})\\cdot 2x)}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{({x}^{2}+5)(6{x}^{2})}{1+4{x}^{6}}-2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}))}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+5)(1+4{x}^{6})}-\\frac{2x}{({x}^{2}+5)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296730 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297414 1069 "q-314762" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297414 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297414 1072 #uuid "8b48b719-9f12-5c32-8d2e-c9f7b2979bfe" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297414 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{6}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\,(2-3{x}^{2})\\,{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}(2-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we ignored the negative square root since $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}\\,(2-3\\,{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}){e}^{-1/(1/2)^{2}} \\\\  & =128\\,(2-\\frac{3}{4}){e}^{-4} \\\\  & =160{e}^{-4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\,(2-3)\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}},$ we conclude that $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296953 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297004 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297415 1069 "q-314779" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297415 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297415 1072 #uuid "9ab06a73-84ed-5847-9d99-435dcc72108e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297415 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$\n\n- setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296653 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297416 1069 "q-31478" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297416 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297416 1072 #uuid "cb025f0b-ba4d-525b-a215-9bdb02362821" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297416 1074 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={5}^{x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,\\text{d}u={5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{5}^{x}+1}\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{5}^{x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that ${5}^{x}+1>0$ for all $x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296898 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297417 1069 "q-31480" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297417 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297417 1072 #uuid "fa16c83a-9f14-57ce-8cd3-45bb3a59d16f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297417 1074 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={4}^{x}+2.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}u={4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{4}^{x}}{{4}^{x}+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{4}^{x}+2}\\cdot {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{4}^{x}+2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x}+2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that ${4}^{x}+2>0$ for all $x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296923 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297418 1069 "q-314895" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297418 1071 "An environmental researcher is tracking the depth $d$, in meters, of a lake during a dry season. The following table shows the rate at which the depth is decreasing.\n\n| **$t\\,\\text{(weeks)}$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${d}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{(meters/week)}$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.8$ | $-1.0$ |\n\nAt $t=2,$ the lake depth was $12$ meters. Use the tangent line approximation of $d$ at time $t=2$ to estimate the depth of the lake at $t=2.4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297418 1072 #uuid "ce7b136c-2ab3-5bfd-98fd-46fda48c1806" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297418 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $d(t)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =d(a)+{d}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =d(2)+{d}^{′}(2)(t-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $d(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $t=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(2.4-2) \\\\  & =12+(-0.5)(0.4) \\\\  & =12-0.2 \\\\  & =11.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe depth of the lake at $t=2.4$ weeks is estimated to be $11.8$ meters." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296707 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297419 1069 "q-314897" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297419 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(5x+1)$ and $f(1)=3.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(1.3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(1.3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297419 1072 #uuid "e790773f-e177-5acb-9017-08e906ced547" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297419 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)=3,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}(5x+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)=(1)^{2}(5\\cdot 1+1)=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=3+6(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.3) & ≈L(1.3) \\\\  & =3+6(1.3-1) \\\\  & =3+6(0.3) \\\\  & =3+1.8 \\\\  & =4.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296910 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297420 1069 "q-314898" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297420 1071 "![](images/d2/d27b0d70171683240fd44c8bbbcc8cb46ce0f0f216533a8061d31a991cce4e14.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=1$ and $x=3.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297420 1072 #uuid "4894e4a4-bc69-5cbe-8eef-c136c4c17945" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297420 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=2$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2+1)-f(2-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296838 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297421 1069 "q-314900" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297421 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297421 1072 #uuid "69493ee6-a225-5106-b233-5e8265954b40" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297421 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈L(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{30} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296646 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296962 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297422 1069 "q-314901" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297422 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=1.5,$ estimate the value of $f(1.7)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n$\\,f(1.7)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297422 1072 #uuid "bcae7982-6c54-5037-8e9f-cce17a6b5d63" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297422 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(2,3),$ so we have $f(2)=3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=1.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+1.5(x-2) \\\\  & =3+1.5x-3 \\\\  & =1.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1.7)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.7.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.7) & ≈L(1.7) \\\\  & =1.5(1.7) \\\\  & =2.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{underestimate}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296899 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297423 1069 "q-31517" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297423 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297423 1072 #uuid "6c446587-25a8-5d97-80ba-a22c604d7eae" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297423 1074 "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297088 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297424 1069 "q-315191" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297424 1071 "Consider the curve ${y}^{2}+2y=-x.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297424 1072 #uuid "c262e9df-8074-5622-af21-290836624895" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297424 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =-x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =-x \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(2\\cdot (-1)+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297145 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297425 1069 "q-315235" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297425 1071 "In a lab experiment, the temperature of a chemical reactor (in ${}^{∘}C$) after $t$ minutes is given by the function $T(t).$ The values of $T(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.2$ | $1.6$ | $2.0$ | $2.4$ | $2.8$ | $3.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$T(t)$** | $28$ | $30$ | $32$ | $34$ | $35$ | $36$ |\n\nApproximate the reactor’s temperature instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.0$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.4.$\n\nThe reactor’s temperature at $t=2.0$ is changing at approximately \n{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}C$ per minute." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297425 1072 #uuid "6df32816-aa0b-54c8-8b61-f33f7d8c1d99" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297425 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{T}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{T(t+h)-T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat time $t=2.0$ with step size $h=0.4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(2.0) & ≈\\frac{T(2.0+0.4)-T(2.0-0.4)}{2(0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(2.4)-T(1.6)}{0.8} \\\\  & =\\frac{34-30}{0.8} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.8} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the reactor’s temperature at $t=2.0$ is changing at approximately $5\\,{}^{∘}C$ per minute." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296918 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297426 1069 "q-315535" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297426 1071 "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}{e}^{3t-3},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Calculate the acceleration of the particle at $t=1$ second.\n\n$a(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297426 1072 #uuid "9324cb86-9675-58ed-95c7-a86964be12d5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297426 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}{e}^{3t-3}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t-3}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{3t-3} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}(1+t){e}^{3t-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration when $t=1$ second, we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =3(1)^{2}(1+1){e}^{3(1)-3} \\\\  & =3(1)(2){e}^{0} \\\\  & =6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297091 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297427 1069 "q-315537" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297427 1071 "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}.$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nWhen $a=\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ the velocity is $v=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297427 1072 #uuid "982e20b4-2713-5f99-b8d4-88200407edc8" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297427 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t+1}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2t+1}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2t+1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7}} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7} & =2t+1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+t+7})}^{2} & ={(2t+1)}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+7 & =4{t}^{2}+4t+1 \\\\ 0 & =3{t}^{2}+3t-6 \\\\ 0 & ={t}^{2}+t-2 \\\\ 0 & =(t-1)(t+2) \\\\ t & =1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $t=1$ since $t\\ge 0.$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=1,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+1+7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+7} \\\\  & =3\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=3\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296742 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296905 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297428 1069 "q-315550" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297428 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(4t-7){e}^{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297428 1072 #uuid "b9d543bb-496c-551f-8dd1-0746724816e3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297428 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-7){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-7){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t} & >0 \\\\ 4t-3 & >0 \\\\ t & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296900 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297070 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297429 1069 "q-315553" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297429 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(5t-15){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297429 1072 #uuid "c8714ea4-94b7-5bd7-91a6-d8cae2e7451c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297429 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-15){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-15){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-10){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-10){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-10){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-5){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5t-5){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ 5t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=(5\\cdot 1-10){e}^{1}=-5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=1,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-5e.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296909 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297430 1069 "q-315645" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297430 1071 "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x-1}+2$ and the point $P(4,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297430 1072 #uuid "216a702f-5f42-58dd-9454-ac0d6b05faab" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297430 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x-1}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{4x-1}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{4x-1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+4x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+4x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(2)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}-4\\,(2)+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4-8+15} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296798 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297431 1069 "q-315648" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297431 1071 "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297431 1072 #uuid "86ce7450-af50-5572-9488-6859cae3ad95" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297431 1074 "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (4t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =16t+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297099 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297432 1069 "q-315674" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297432 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=6{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the time when the particle's acceleration is equal to $a=150\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe acceleration is $150\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at\n$t=$\n{{field-1}} seconds." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297432 1072 #uuid "ca2af60f-d8cc-5c4a-82da-4cf0075e53e1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297432 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(18{t}^{2}+6t) \\\\  & =36t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=150,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150 & =36t+6 \\\\ 36t & =144 \\\\ t & =4\\text{ }\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is $150\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at $t=4$ seconds." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296728 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297433 1069 "q-315701" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297433 1071 "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-4)$ between $x=2$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{3}{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡($\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297433 1072 #uuid "ed8b4ad4-c2df-5a80-8184-0f46540123d3" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297433 1074 "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=2$ and $b=5,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(2+\\frac{3j}{n})-4) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{3}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296855 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297434 1069 "q-315717" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297434 1071 "The area under the curve $y=(x+2)^{3/2}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $10$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j.$*\n\n${S}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}$\n ${\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}($\n{{field-1}}$)^{3/2}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297434 1072 #uuid "c1487daa-866b-50a7-afd5-9a9671f89a9a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297434 1074 "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're given that there are  $n=10$ subintervals.  The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$  so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{10}=\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{10-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}f(\\frac{2j-4}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{((-2+\\frac{j}{2})+2)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{9}{(\\frac{j}{2})}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296988 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297435 1069 "q-315735" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297435 1071 "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=2$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297435 1072 #uuid "ce341729-3a59-5798-9fad-f886c34df302" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297435 1074 "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t+\\frac{4}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+\\frac{4}{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =2-\\frac{4}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & =ma(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297027 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297436 1069 "q-315743" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297436 1071 "The following table shows the inflow rate of water, $q(t),$ in $\\text{L/min},$ into a storage tank over a period of $90$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $20$ | $60$ | $90$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)\\,(\\text{L/min})$ | $15$ | $25$ | $20$ | $10$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,20]\\cup [20,60]\\cup [60,90],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total volume of water that flowed into the tank in $90$ minutes.\n\nThe total volume is {{field-1}} liters." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297436 1072 #uuid "d72b5812-f9a9-5509-bb0d-9bac0079a069" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297436 1074 "In this context, we have $3$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=q(t)$ represents the total volume of water that flowed into the tank in $90$ minutes.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{q(0)+q(20)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{q(20)+q(60)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}+\\frac{q(60)+q(90)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{15+25}{2}\\cdot (20-0)+\\frac{25+20}{2}\\cdot (60-20)+\\frac{20+10}{2}\\cdot (90-60) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 20+22.5\\cdot 40+15\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =400+900+450 \\\\  & =1750.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total volume of water that flowed into the tank is $1750$ liters." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296695 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297437 1069 "q-315824" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297437 1071 "![](images/8b/8b8ca01e64d2830bd5fce6a5c0b47b74f955ae1e4a7e37dcd0c7bcaa41ed1383.png)\n\nA small, circular ink blot spreads on a paper. If the blot’s circumference is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$, calculate the rate of change of its area at the moment when the radius is $10\\,\\text{mm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe area of the ink blot is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297437 1072 #uuid "34fe7280-76bb-5067-ad59-9c70ad7f535a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297437 1074 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=10,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=10\\,\\text{mm},$ $C=2\\pi (10)=20\\pi \\text{ mm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi $ and $C=20\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{20\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area is increasing at a rate of $40\\pi {\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296967 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297438 1069 "q-315830" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297438 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297438 1072 #uuid "3d16d840-e772-54e8-bd9e-bcce6a940ff4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297438 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=9{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296937 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297439 1069 "q-31585" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297439 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297439 1072 #uuid "dc410ce1-0fb1-54b6-a7ff-4bcc2c871ce4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297439 1074 "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{x}-x-1]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1} & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{x}-1]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296740 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297440 1069 "q-315860" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297440 1071 "![](images/e8/e850cd14f3b4f791c79c136d0fc799fd17fdc23eeab37e67ad2811f3ff7b6d0e.png)\n\nA snowball is melting in the sun and slowly shrinking. Determine the rate at which the surface area of the snowball is decreasing, given that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s}$ and its surface area at that moment is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297440 1072 #uuid "cb5b4de1-d892-5870-99f2-1c26e26d0697" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297440 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296848 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297441 1069 "q-315984" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297441 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297441 1072 #uuid "719a311f-6c8f-513c-84dc-85675a6f24ea" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297441 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{4} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296840 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297442 1069 "q-315985" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297442 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297442 1072 #uuid "41aa5101-c6e0-5e2e-af55-4009a409fb57" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297442 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{-2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{2\\cdot {3}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297142 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297443 1069 "q-31599" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297443 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297443 1072 #uuid "d2561bb5-3392-587d-bd91-b80052a1b14b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297443 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0-{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2(0)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297161 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297444 1069 "q-31600" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297444 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297444 1072 #uuid "e847ac9b-6147-5e36-9747-365456716188" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297444 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=-1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+3x+2]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{x}{x+2})}{2x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1+2)+\\frac{-1}{-1+2})}{2(-1)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-1}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297096 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297445 1069 "q-316004" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297445 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297445 1072 #uuid "7f1d1b78-a91f-5b2c-9c58-c656ac210846" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297445 1074 "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(({e}^{2})^{3}-{1}^{3})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{6}-1)-(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297122 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297446 1069 "q-316014" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297446 1071 "Find the value of $a$ that minimizes the distance between the points $(a,\\,-2a)$ and $(4,\\,-3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297446 1072 #uuid "7745e746-bc78-5626-875d-e5cef20486d7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297446 1074 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a-4)^{2}+((-2a)-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(a-4)^{2}+(-2a+3)^{2} \\\\  & =({a}^{2}-8a+16)+(4{a}^{2}-12a+9) \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-20a+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S$, we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=10a-20.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-20 & =0 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(a)=10>0.$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=2.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296960 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297447 1069 "q-31602" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297447 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}=$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297447 1072 #uuid "d4cee0a4-4d54-5d5b-88b7-af1da57b4e05" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297447 1074 "Note that plugging in $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{-2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296690 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297448 1069 "q-316022" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297448 1071 "![](images/b5/b5432ed129d85a3718f8e5ebe0cca141731c366d1b32e8e72d0916c60c34de2c.png)\n\nA region in the $x$-$y$ plane is bounded by the curve $y={sec}^{2}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}$, and the $x$-axis between $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, as shown above. \nFind the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297448 1072 #uuid "96fe6acf-39fc-5928-b3e7-6b1c83c18a47" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297448 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sec}^{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)){|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi /3}{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296837 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297449 1069 "q-316023" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297449 1071 "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0),$ where $b$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297449 1072 #uuid "1881e344-7776-5325-86c9-42068cf4edbb" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297449 1074 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & ={(b-3)}^{2}+{(2b-0)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b)^{2} \\\\  & =({b}^{2}-6b+9)+(4{b}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-6b+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(b)=10b-6.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-6 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}\\,(\\frac{3}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(b)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $b=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-6(\\frac{3}{5})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}-\\frac{18}{5}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0)$ is $\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297017 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297450 1069 "q-316024" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297450 1071 "![](images/d2/d282e4afd0a66dd79fecf474da9f618a5970197943044d24208f86c4c43781e6.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297450 1072 #uuid "3e8278e6-1f98-55c4-88ff-9d0d17e17bd5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297450 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x{|}_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =-(arcsin\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-arcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296890 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297451 1069 "q-316032" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297451 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by the equation $s(t)=2{t}^{4}-3t+5$, where $t\\ge 0$ is measured in seconds. At how many seconds is the particle's velocity equal to $509\\,\\text{m/s}?$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297451 1072 #uuid "d10d4b36-d006-5579-8361-610a3f33cdbe" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297451 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-3t+5) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to know when the velocity is $509\\,\\text{m/s}$, we substitute $v(t)=509$ into the equation above and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}509 & =8{t}^{3}-3 \\\\ 512 & =8{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =\\frac{512}{8} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =64 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle is $509\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296697 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297452 1069 "q-316062" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297452 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in centimeters) at time $t$ minutes is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=3{t}^{6}-2{t}^{4}-8{t}^{2}-7t,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=1\\,\\text{min}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{cm/min}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297452 1072 #uuid "d9d6033d-a8e4-50b8-ac74-48f6972cb023" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297452 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{6}-2{t}^{4}-8{t}^{2}-7t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{5}-8{t}^{3}-16t-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=1,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =18(1)^{5}-8(1)^{3}-16(1)-7 \\\\  & =18-8-16-7 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-13|=13\\,\\text{cm/min}.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296724 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297453 1069 "q-316094" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297453 1071 "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-2}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297453 1072 #uuid "9d548dc0-e313-51f4-a971-aa08e7922149" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297453 1074 "Notice that the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-2}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296699 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297454 1069 "q-316099" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297454 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-3$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{1}^{7}(2-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{7}(2-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297454 1072 #uuid "b26001df-6869-5f80-8e5e-d59b11ee9816" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297454 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n(-3)+8\\,=\\,{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{7}(2-3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{1}^{7}1\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,3{\\int }_{1}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot x{|}_{1}^{7}\\,-\\,3(5) \\\\  & =2(7-1)-15 \\\\  & =12-15 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297129 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297455 1069 "q-316122" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297455 1071 "![](images/64/64ce1ae5f198ce41e55414d853b1bd99e73d747cddb80412b30246bfdb13a907.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297455 1072 #uuid "c6302582-812c-5f14-9dac-2aabed1d56fa" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297455 1074 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,8+6 \\\\  & =\\,14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297134 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297456 1069 "q-316128" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297456 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297456 1072 #uuid "05eac531-b76e-5049-a87c-d2e382ce576e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297456 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}x{e}^{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297007 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297457 1069 "q-316132" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297457 1071 "![](images/b8/b871a949beaac0319d77364c43aa261bda8c28642f8bfc80a80f974fd7323cf3.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297457 1072 #uuid "cbb60ffa-14d6-5751-913f-1131a36b5e4d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297457 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-2+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296645 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297458 1069 "q-316137" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297458 1071 "![](images/3a/3aa68477e9e8f3bc688981cbd227e21af798364b8c0334ee9a22163b400b4064.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{a}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{a}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297458 1072 #uuid "3b90bcf5-c930-580a-b51e-8cb14af68bee" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297458 1074 "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{4} & =9 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{(x-1)^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{9} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm {9}^{1/4}\\,=\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-2$ and $x=0$ is $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSince $1-\\sqrt{3}\\in [-2,0],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1-\\sqrt{3}$ to $x=0.$\n\nSo, $a=1-\\sqrt{3}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{1-\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}(\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{4}-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297031 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297459 1069 "q-316149" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297459 1071 "Calculate $F(4)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297459 1072 #uuid "422c75c5-d873-55ce-a685-2e3417a764ad" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297459 1074 "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=4,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(4)={\\int }_{0}^{4}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297029 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297460 1069 "q-31619" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297460 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/5}^{{e}^{4}/5}\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297460 1072 #uuid "e4974d46-0f4e-5eb4-8954-be690c361878" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297460 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x).$ Then  we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5x}\\cdot 5=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/5$ | ${e}^{4}/5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e/5}^{{e}^{4}/5}\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{e/5}^{{e}^{4}/5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{1/2}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {\\sqrt{u}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6(\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{1}) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296748 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297461 1069 "q-316226" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297461 1071 "![](images/0a/0a7a32f856d63403d7125f1c6bb9acc9a5fcd182c2f7fbd3f3ebdfa42f9c9c2c.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}-8$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ (shown above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297461 1072 #uuid "cd6c73c9-bd3c-53be-accb-e4b86f6cd635" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297461 1074 "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-8{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-8\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3}-0)-8(2-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}-16 \\\\  & =-\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{56}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297084 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297462 1069 "q-316254" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297462 1071 "In $2005$, a group of scientists started tracking the population of sea turtles in a coastal sanctuary. The initial population was $180,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $4+1.5t$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2005.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of sea turtles in the sanctuary at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297462 1072 #uuid "d8f85d18-c70d-5c3e-acb8-1dd6b68eae84" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297462 1074 "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)={{4+1.5t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(1+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=t+3.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of turtles $T$ years after $2005.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(t+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =180+\\frac{1}{2}{T}^{2}+3T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=180+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296999 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297463 1069 "q-316261" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297463 1071 "![](images/d3/d36f2859865da58d226a738d8338f726df950db75b7421b604bb692c9049d54a.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297463 1072 #uuid "53c80807-8464-558f-b4e9-61601f18a79a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297463 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296771 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297464 1069 "q-316302" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297464 1071 "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)={cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $[0,\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297464 1072 #uuid "a9a63b25-eb4e-543f-9169-7fae6ed27cad" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297464 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this integral using a substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{{u}^{9}}{9})|}_{1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }[(-1)^{9}-{1}^{9}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296882 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297465 1069 "q-316303" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297465 1071 "Find the value of $c$ such that the average value of $h(x)=10x-4$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $6.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297465 1072 #uuid "f0bab2f3-434f-5bc7-bbe6-28166bd448b1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297465 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{c}(10x-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c}(5{x}^{2}-4x){|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(5{c}^{2}-4c)-0] \\\\ 6 & =5c-4 \\\\ 10 & =5c \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296727 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296752 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297466 1069 "q-316306" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297466 1071 "In **ascending order**, the **exact** nonzero values of $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=12{x}^{2}-16x-12$ over the interval with endpoints $0$ and $c$ is equal to $0$ are\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and  $\\,c=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297466 1072 #uuid "99a24adb-6708-51b8-b0d4-65107179bc32" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297466 1074 "To solve for $c\\ne 0,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(12{x}^{2}-16x-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & ={\\frac{1}{c}(4{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}-12x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(4{c}^{3}-8{c}^{2}-12c)-0] \\\\ 0 & =4{c}^{2}-8c-12 \\\\ 0 & =4({c}^{2}-2c-3) \\\\ 0 & =4(c-3)(c+1) \\\\ c & =-1,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in **ascending order**, the values of $c$ are \n\n$$\nc=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,c=3.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297026 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297467 1069 "q-316307" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297467 1071 "![](images/64/64682c2166e3f9fd2366f925330d04f95b54f370fd3d887d1d8aef8885ac35ec.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-8,4],$ is shown above. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $20$, $80$ and $10,$ respectively (from left to right). \n\nFind the average value of $f(x)$ on $[-8,4].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297467 1072 #uuid "4e9e13fd-8e57-57f8-962c-b25ad48b691a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297467 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval is\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, we see that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-8}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-20+80-10=50.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the average value formula, we find that the average value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-8)}\\cdot (50) \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297143 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297468 1069 "q-316309" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297468 1071 "![](images/1c/1c69b5a43e445861a728fcd8faed9e89b3bcb61141ad35d99e60f2a43983244d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-2,9],$ is shown above. It consists of three line segments and the arc of the semi-circle centered at $(5,0)$ with radius $3.$ \n\nFind the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of $f(x)$ on $[0,8].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297468 1072 #uuid "4e5a2c9b-15eb-580e-9b48-bc6a0c515d66" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297468 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,8].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8-0}\\cdot (-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi -4}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297139 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297469 1069 "q-316342" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297469 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297469 1072 #uuid "8b45d88b-4305-5382-91bc-deddd0be51bb" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297469 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{4}=2,\\,b=\\sqrt{49}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{7}{2}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{7}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296867 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297470 1069 "q-316345" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297470 1071 "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨-3,5,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,-1,2⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297470 1072 #uuid "e9963779-43cf-5b65-8821-6ff0b678d8cf" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297470 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))i-((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =10i+6j-17k \\\\  & =⟨10,6,-17⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-17)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+36+289} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{425} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297153 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297471 1069 "q-316350" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297471 1071 "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨1,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨0,3,4⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297471 1072 #uuid "5b9773af-f53a-5ffd-bd89-0fdc3a8398e6" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297471 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =0i-4j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296904 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297472 1069 "q-316355" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297472 1071 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $p=⟨0,5,-1⟩$ and $q=⟨6,0,1⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297472 1072 #uuid "ed3e1530-1ab9-5085-9128-11a28820fcba" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297472 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\times q & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 6)j+(0\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 6)k \\\\  & =5i-6j-30k \\\\  & =⟨5,-6,-30⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,p\\times q\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-30)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+36+900} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{961} \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{31}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296676 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297473 1069 "q-316361" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297473 1071 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,PQR$, if $P(1,-2,0)$, $Q(3,1,-1)$, $R(-1,0,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297473 1072 #uuid "2e86dffd-3cdd-51b5-8002-a730ad7bd647" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297473 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(3i+j-k)-(i-2j) \\\\  & =2i+3j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(-i+2k)-(i-2j) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)i-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2))j+(2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =8i-2j+10k \\\\  & =⟨8,-2,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+4+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{168} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{42}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{42}}{2}=\\sqrt{42}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296896 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297474 1069 "q-316362" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297474 1071 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,DEF$, if $D(-2,1,3)$, $E(0,-1,0)$, $F(2,2,-1).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297474 1072 #uuid "a38f6d6d-8117-5b90-a022-c6e9c7160729" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297474 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(-j)-(-2i+j+3k) \\\\  & =2i-2j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(2i+2j-k)-(-2i+j+3k) \\\\  & =4i+j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 4)j+(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =11i-4j+10k \\\\  & =⟨11,-4,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{11}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{237}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{237}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296946 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297475 1069 "q-316363" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297475 1071 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(-3,0,1)$, $M(0,2,-2)$, $N(2,-1,0).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297475 1072 #uuid "66634719-8e4b-59db-b970-ace87e066b21" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297475 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2j-2k)-(-3i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j-3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(2i-j)-(-3i+k) \\\\  & =5i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨5,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))i-(3\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot 5)j+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-5i-12j-13k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-12,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-12)^{2}+(-13)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144+169} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{338} \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297090 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297476 1069 "q-316373" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297476 1071 "If $x<2,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=${{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297476 1072 #uuid "573453db-0716-5078-8c45-8f1ef4caf3c5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297476 1074 "Here, the variable limit $x$ appears as the lower limit of integration. To apply FTC II directly, we prefer the variable on the upper limit. Using the property of definite integrals, we rewrite\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow, by FTC II we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297128 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297477 1069 "q-316389" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297477 1071 "Let $f(t)={t}^{2}{e}^{3t}.$ Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<3.$\n\n${h}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297477 1072 #uuid "51c3f5a9-6c27-5ba4-9095-797b362115e5" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297477 1074 "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we prefer the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{3}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{3}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-{2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296767 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297478 1069 "q-31639" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297478 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{1}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297478 1072 #uuid "a9ace025-d2a7-57e0-b56c-f814cc5835a7" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297478 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | ${e}^{2}$ | ${e}^{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{1}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{{e}^{2}}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296915 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297479 1069 "q-316392" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297479 1071 "Calculate the length of the altitude $NP$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(3,-1,0)$, $M(0,2,1)$, $N(-2,1,-1).$\n\n$NP=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297479 1072 #uuid "cc3dd100-4787-576c-a303-83ee06838d2b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297479 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $NP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $NP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2j+k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-3i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(-2i+j-k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-5i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2)i-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-5))j+((-3)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-5))k \\\\  & =-5i-8j+9k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-8,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ \\sqrt{170} & =NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ NP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296957 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297480 1069 "q-316395" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297480 1071 "Calculate the length of the altitude $UT$ of the $△\\,UVW$, if $U(-2,2,0)$, $V(1,-1,2)$, $W(3,0,-1).$\n\n$UT=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297480 1072 #uuid "118c9278-fa2c-5ecd-aadf-b52c033ba66c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297480 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $UT$ can be related to the area of $△\\,UVW$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $UT,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW} & =w-v \\\\  & =(3i-k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =u-v \\\\  & =(-2i+2j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdo