\frac{1}{2}\\,IP\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\sqrt{2}\\,IP \\\\ IP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296908 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297484 1069 "q-31643" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297484 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297484 1072 #uuid "5f3473f0-efaa-5747-926c-a19ff897e0a6" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297484 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x}\\cdot 2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/2$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{1/2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\sqrt{u}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{1}-\\sqrt{0}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297025 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297485 1069 "q-316436" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297485 1071 "Suppose that $|\\,m\\,|=12$, $|\\,n\\,|=9$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={30}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $m$ onto $n$ in terms of the vector $n.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{n}\\,m=$\n{{field-1}}\n$n$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297485 1072 #uuid "40364043-8ec3-55bb-806d-beaf05528093" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297485 1074 "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m & =|\\,m\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $n:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{n}{|\\,n\\,|}=\\frac{n}{9}=\\frac{1}{9}n\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{n}\\,m & ={\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m\\cdot u \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}n \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297158 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297486 1069 "q-316441" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297486 1071 "Suppose that $|\\,a\\,|=4$, $|\\,b\\,|=8\\sqrt{2}$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the vector projection of $b$ onto $a$ in terms of the vector $a.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,b=$\n{{field-1}}\n$a$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297486 1072 #uuid "7177af1c-e318-563b-be13-170617488953" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297486 1074 "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $a:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}=\\frac{a}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}a\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,b & ={\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b\\cdot u \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}a \\\\  & =2\\,a\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296830 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296842 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296916 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1069 "q-316469" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1071 "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d=⟨$  \n\n{{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}},\n\n{{field-3}}\n\n$⟩$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1072 #uuid "11a02f05-1ba9-5780-8f1e-0d85159aa8b9" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297487 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{c\\cdot c}c \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(0)+(-2)(5)+(1)(-1)}{{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{0-10-1}{9+4+1}\\,⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{14}\\,⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{33}{14},\\frac{11}{7},-\\frac{11}{14}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n`" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296825 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297488 1069 "q-31648" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297488 1071 "What is the value of  ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{3{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\sqrt{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297488 1072 #uuid "8f30c06d-366c-59ac-9244-d2bac8160fec" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297488 1074 "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{3{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\sqrt{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot (16-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297113 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297489 1069 "q-316483" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297489 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297489 1072 #uuid "c61b2dc3-a765-5083-a01c-48eaa7b46451" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297489 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{49}=7,\\,b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{7}x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296796 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297490 1069 "q-316489" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297490 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297490 1072 #uuid "688c2800-c6d3-56c2-94f7-f495f76bcf20" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297490 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{20},\\,b=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{10}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296952 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297491 1069 "q-316506" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297491 1071 "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297491 1072 #uuid "1c4d4c03-9fe4-577e-8d85-9702fdb8c3cf" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297491 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{36}=6,\\,b=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{6}{2}x \\\\  & =\\pm 3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296959 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297492 1069 "q-316514" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297492 1071 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,DEF$, if $D(-1,2,0)$, $E(2,0,1)$, $F(1,3,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297492 1072 #uuid "1c80aa6a-7a46-5942-80d2-93913627438b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297492 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(2i+k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =3i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(i+3j+2k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =2i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)i-(3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-5i-4j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-4,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{90} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297141 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297493 1069 "q-316516" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297493 1071 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the $△\\,LMN$, if $L(1,0,-1)$, $M(3,2,0)$, $N(0,1,3).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297493 1072 #uuid "af1c1af8-7ccd-5f01-9374-729457ea6109" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297493 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(3i+2j)-(i-k) \\\\  & =2i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(j+3k)-(i-k) \\\\  & =-i+j+4k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =7i-9j+4k \\\\  & =⟨7,-9,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{146}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296968 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297494 1069 "q-31653" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297494 1071 "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1},\\,{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297494 1072 #uuid "17401c5c-84e7-5182-8398-ec9e279cae2f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297494 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 2t,3{t}^{2}-12t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},3t(t-4)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0, \\\\ 3t(t-4)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=0,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=0$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the object is stationary." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296757 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297495 1069 "q-316530" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297495 1071 "Calculate the area of the $△\\,STU,$ if $S(0,1,2),$ $T(2,3,1),$ $U(4,1,2).$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297495 1072 #uuid "cf87a92d-df17-5548-a069-1e61f18f426a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297495 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =t-s \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =u-s \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(j+2k) \\\\  & =4i+0j+0k \\\\  & =⟨4,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 4)j+(2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =0i-4j-8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,STU$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{2}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296877 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297496 1069 "q-31654" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297496 1071 "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,2{t}^{2}+6t-3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297496 1072 #uuid "82b265db-264e-5810-9acb-572bc78fdb47" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297496 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,4t+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3({t}^{2}-1),2(2t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t-1)(t+1)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ 2(2t+3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is no value of $t$ that satisfies both equations, so there are no stationary points." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297159 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297497 1069 "q-316553" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297497 1071 "Calculate the length of the altitude $CK$ of the $△\\,ABC,$ if $A(1,0,1),$ $B(4,2,1),$ $C(2,3,2).$\n\n$CK=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297497 1072 #uuid "3aadd472-6c53-5587-bcdd-18195d95d688" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297497 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $CK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+2j+k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+0k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+2k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-3j+7k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296851 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297498 1069 "q-316556" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297498 1071 "Calculate the length of the altitude $HK$ of the $△\\,GHI,$ if $G(1,1,1),$ $H(3,2,4),$ $I(2,4,2).$\n\n$HK=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297498 1072 #uuid "9fd89971-85c9-5581-b57b-a897961ff335" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297498 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $HK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GHI$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI\n$$\n\nSo, to find $HK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI} & =i-g \\\\  & =(2i+4j+2k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =h-g \\\\  & =(3i+2j+4k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨8,-1,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $GI$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GI & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296801 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297499 1069 "q-316558" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297499 1071 "Calculate the length of the altitude $TK$ of the $△\\,RST,$ if $R(4,5,6),$ $S(7,4,5),$ $T(6,8,4).$\n\n$TK=${{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297499 1072 #uuid "05e91010-8b10-50cc-b3a2-21f43d310a7e" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297499 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $TK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,RST$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS\n$$\n\nSo, to find $TK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =s-r \\\\  & =(7i+4j+5k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =3i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =t-r \\\\  & =(6i+8j+4k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)i-(3(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =5i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{162} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $RS$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296701 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297500 1069 "q-316567" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297500 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297500 1072 #uuid "b409383e-6fc2-599c-a9ff-f2d3f2066a9b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297500 1074 "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|6\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 4| \\\\  & =|18-20| \\\\  & =|-2| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296845 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297501 1069 "q-316569" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297501 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297501 1072 #uuid "4c2b63b5-8e9a-53f8-91df-f96b487d39a4" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297501 1074 "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|-10\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|-80-9| \\\\  & =|-89| \\\\  & =89.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296866 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297502 1069 "q-316570" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297502 1071 "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -10 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." 13194139533411 17592186045585