\  & =5({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}-12x+9x+18) \\\\  & =5({x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-3x+18) \\\\  & =5{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}-15x+90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=5{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}-15x+90.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296815 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296949 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297065 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1069 "q-316630" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=0,$ ${x}_{2}=-4,$ and ${x}_{3}=7,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-5.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1072 #uuid "1d2013b2-8494-5125-8934-79d3fbc59093" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297530 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=0,$ ${x}_{2}=-4,$ and ${x}_{3}=7,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-5,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-5(x-0)(x-(-4))(x-7) \\\\  & =-5\\,x\\,(x+4)(x-7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\,x\\,(x+4)(x-7) & =-5\\,x\\,({x}^{2}-3x-28) \\\\  & =-5({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-28x) \\\\  & =-5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}+140x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}+140x.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296737 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296894 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297009 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297010 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1069 "q-316632" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=1,$ ${x}_{2}=-5,$ and ${x}_{3}=-2,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-4.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1072 #uuid "e449e2db-1584-5090-8f71-7510ec032bf2" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297531 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=1,$ ${x}_{2}=-5,$ and ${x}_{3}=-2,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-4(x-1)(x-(-5))(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =-4(x-1)(x+5)(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(x-1)(x+5)(x+2) & =-4({x}^{2}+4x-5)(x+2) \\\\  & =-4({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}+8x-5x-10) \\\\  & =-4({x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+3x-10) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}-12x+40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-4{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}-12x+40.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296881 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296906 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296973 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296980 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1069 "q-316635" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=5,{x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-2,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-4.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1072 #uuid "fba2441c-e2d8-5707-adb2-486b08741af1" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297532 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-2,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-4(x-5)(x-(-2))(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =-4(x-5)(x+2)(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(x-5)(x+2)(x+2) & =-4(x-5)({x}^{2}+4x+4) \\\\  & =-4({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+4x-5{x}^{2}-20x-20) \\\\  & =-4({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-16x-20) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+64x+80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+64x+80.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296663 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296811 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297001 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297103 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1069 "q-316646" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2,{x}_{2,3}=\\pm 4\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $3.$ Find $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1072 #uuid "3731cd34-f150-5722-ad6e-69edbb9e6285" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297533 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 4\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =3(x-(-2))(x-4\\text{i})(x-(-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(x+2)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+2)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i}) & = \\\\ 3(x+2)({x}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ 3(x+2)({x}^{2}+16) & = \\\\ 3({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+16x+32) & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+48x+96 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+48x+96.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296754 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296841 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297002 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297050 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1069 "q-316649" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,{x}_{2,3}=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $5.$ Find $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1072 #uuid "aec895e8-bc3e-50c1-904e-c5d1b4fa0402" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297534 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=5,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =5(x-(-5))(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =5(x+5)(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x+5)(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ 5(x+5)({x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ 5(x+5)({x}^{2}+4) & = \\\\ 5({x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+4x+20) & = \\\\ 5{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}+20x+100 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}+20x+100.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296677 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296765 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297014 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297064 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1069 "q-316652" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=3,{x}_{2,3}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $6.$ Find $f(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1072 #uuid "6cdd99a5-da9f-5683-9b18-848bbade62d2" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297535 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2,3}=1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=6,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =6(x-3)(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x-3)(x-(1+2\\text{i}))(x-(1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 6(x-3)({x}^{2}-x(1+2\\text{i})-x(1-2\\text{i})+(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 6(x-3)({x}^{2}-2x+5) & = \\\\ 6({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+11x-15) & = \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+66x-90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+66x-90.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296884 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296920 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297021 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297138 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1069 "q-316656" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=5,{x}_{2,3}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-4.$ Find $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1072 #uuid "10231f19-13c6-5529-abc7-f00f49668d0b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297536 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2,3}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4(x-5)(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-4(x-5)(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(x-5)(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -4(x-5)({x}^{2}-x(-1-2\\text{i})-x(-1+2\\text{i})+(-1+2\\text{i})(-1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -4(x-5)({x}^{2}+2x+5) & = \\\\ -4({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+5x-5{x}^{2}-10x-25) & = \\\\ -4({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-5x-25) & = \\\\ -4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+20x+100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+20x+100.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296795 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296926 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296995 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297144 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1069 "q-316689" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1071 "![](images/b2/b2dbc407d70f4e6d3cf35dd24e1a6fb7d463207631a0498595ed40550ac86433.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a cubic polynomial $f(x).$\nFind $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $-5.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1072 #uuid "8dedb157-2b1f-50bf-ae49-712ca9b20334" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297537 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-5.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-5(x-3)(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-5(x-3)(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(x-3)(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ -5(x-3)({x}^{2}+x(2\\text{i})-x(2\\text{i})-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ -5(x-3)({x}^{2}+4) & = \\\\ -5({x}^{3}+4x-3{x}^{2}-12) & = \\\\ -5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-20x+60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-20x+60.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297072 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297538 1069 "q-31669" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297538 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{7{log}_{5}⁡(5x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297538 1072 #uuid "7168c66b-e063-53c6-92aa-cf6372184dec" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297538 1074 "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡(5x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot 5=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\frac{7{log}_{5}⁡(5x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{1}^{5}{log}_{5}⁡(5x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =7{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot (\\frac{4}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296932 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297539 1069 "q-316692" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297539 1071 "Find the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=2-{y}^{8}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297539 1072 #uuid "24e02d71-25dc-54e7-a4b0-aeb294c81fdf" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297539 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-{y}^{8})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1}\\,-\\,{\\frac{{y}^{9}}{9}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1-0)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{9}-0) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296726 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296800 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296835 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296970 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1069 "q-316704" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1071 "![](images/ce/ce15895aa3a0f7e7cbea820e06a3c56aadef1d440b59120db17c5df394970adb.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a cubic polynomial $f(x).$\nFind $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $-7.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1072 #uuid "dd1fb75a-89ec-5672-ba77-4f8556ccd77f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297540 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-7.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x-(-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(x-1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i}) & = \\\\ -7(x-1)({x}^{2}+x(4\\text{i})-x(4\\text{i})-(4\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ -7(x-1)({x}^{2}+16) & = \\\\ -7({x}^{3}+16x-{x}^{2}-16) & = \\\\ -7{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-112x+112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-7{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-112x+112.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296832 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296888 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296943 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296956 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1069 "q-316707" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=6,\\,{x}_{2,3}=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-4.$ Find $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1072 #uuid "b503e515-255f-56bc-8cff-21d5e4bbae13" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297541 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=6$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4(x-6)(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-4(x-6)(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(x-6)(x-3\\text{i})(x+3\\text{i}) & = \\\\ -4(x-6)({x}^{2}-x(-3\\text{i})-x(3\\text{i})+(3\\text{i})(-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -4(x-6)({x}^{2}+9) & = \\\\ -4({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x-54) & = \\\\ -4{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-36x+216.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-36x+216.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297046 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297542 1069 "q-31671" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297542 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨6t+1,-3{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297542 1072 #uuid "d854fcf1-769c-563b-98b9-fcdfe43a4f80" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297542 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨6,-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨-6,6⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 36} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297068 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297543 1069 "q-316714" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297543 1071 "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x={y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y$ and the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297543 1072 #uuid "c89117c1-0d6b-5b6e-8bf1-11d2608dd39c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297543 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-10y+25) & =0 \\\\ y(y-5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/59/593d1168ce7328aa93edd61fcabc116451fcda8ef24faeac0949fa0a8e686b51.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x={y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=5.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\,({y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}+25y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-10{\\int }_{0}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+25{\\int }_{0}^{5}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}|}_{0}^{5}-{\\frac{10}{3}{y}^{3}|}_{0}^{5}+{\\frac{25}{2}{y}^{2}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{5}^{4}}{4}-0)-\\frac{10}{3}({5}^{3}-0)+\\frac{25}{2}({5}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{4}-\\frac{1250}{3}+\\frac{625}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296755 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296756 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297147 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1069 "q-316725" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1071 "![](images/dd/dd92f4b41c95c436027cce015b688df1a982f46913b0ea262f2d30e1e49259d5.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of the cubic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $-2.$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1072 #uuid "d76039a2-01f1-5d8a-a55a-8960fd4792dd" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297544 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2,3}=-1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-2.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-2(x-2)(x-(-1+3\\text{i}))(x-(-1-3\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x-2)(x-(-1+3\\text{i}))(x-(-1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -2(x-2)({x}^{2}-x(-1-3\\text{i})-x(-1+3\\text{i})+(-1+3\\text{i})(-1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -2(x-2)({x}^{2}+2x+10) & = \\\\ -2({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+10x-2{x}^{2}-4x-20) & = \\\\ -2({x}^{3}+6x-20) & = \\\\ -2{x}^{3}-12x+40 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-2{x}^{3}-12x+40.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296716 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297116 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297545 1069 "q-316736" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297545 1071 "![](images/6c/6c3a45ac200d1f65c326354297e6e03b5347b4d4ec3fbb2a99bb7cde98376db8.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}$ (plotted above) is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297545 1072 #uuid "8116279a-006f-5701-aca9-d9c79bb44b95" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297545 1074 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3x}\\cdot 3)x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))}{{x}^{2}}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$ is the domain of $g$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for $x>0,$ the denominator is positive; thus this reduces to\n\n$$\n1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)<0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)=1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{e}{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, this critical point is a maximum. The slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{e}{3}) & \\frac{e}{3} & (\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ) \\\\ g & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {g}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(\\frac{e}{3},\\infty ).$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296917 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297546 1069 "q-31674" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297546 1071 "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}^{2}+2t,6{t}^{2}-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0.$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297546 1072 #uuid "ac3c1af1-d531-5585-83c9-f48f03ae136d" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297546 1074 "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+2,\\,12t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨2(0)+2,12(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(0)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨2,0⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(0)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296966 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297547 1069 "q-316742" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297547 1071 "![](images/a9/a988c243bcf4b82cd05b1ed0292e0595141dbe37a470abc34b183f0ce184fe3f.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297547 1072 #uuid "f1d1ee30-1190-5b35-acf5-2df3a55be76b" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297547 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8}-2 \\\\ y+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{x+8} \\\\ (y+2)^{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =(y+2)^{3}-8 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{-2}^{0}\\,+\\,6\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{-2}^{0}+12\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{16}{4})+6(0-(-\\frac{8}{3}))+12(0-\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =-4+16-24 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-12|=12\\,.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296723 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297548 1069 "q-316744" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297548 1071 "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}} ." 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297548 1072 #uuid "565c6a17-60a3-559c-9523-dbf71d9612db" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297548 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{y}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296722 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296725 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297111 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1069 "q-316753" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1071 "Calculate ${A}^{-1},$ where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} | $-2$ |  |" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1072 #uuid "5afec83f-c01d-57b0-a129-7a451b173c66" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297549 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(A)=(-6)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-5)=3\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -(-5) & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2/3 & -1 \\\\ 5/3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2/3 & -1 \\\\ 5/3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2/3 & -1 \\\\ 5/3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296734 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296878 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297550 1069 "q-316755" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297550 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{-6+5\\text{i}}{4+7\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297550 1072 #uuid "01844fd5-ba60-50ee-9116-494fb1e9fc7a" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297550 1074 "The denominator is $4+7\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $4-7\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $4-7\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6+5\\text{i}}{4+7\\text{i}} & =\\frac{-6+5\\text{i}}{4+7\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{4-7\\text{i}}{4-7\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6+5\\text{i})(4-7\\text{i})}{(4+7\\text{i})(4-7\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24+42\\text{i}+20\\text{i}-35{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{4}^{2}-(7\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24+62\\text{i}-35(-1)}{{4}^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24+62\\text{i}+35}{16+49} \\\\  & =\\frac{11+62\\text{i}}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11+62\\text{i}}{65}=(\\frac{11}{65})+(\\frac{62}{65})\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297066 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297119 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297551 1069 "q-316756" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297551 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297551 1072 #uuid "8cadbc9e-d855-58f0-9e49-d42247bff4db" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297551 1074 "The denominator is $8-11\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $8+11\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $8+11\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}} & =\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{8+11\\text{i}}{8+11\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12-19\\text{i})(8+11\\text{i})}{(8-11\\text{i})(8+11\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{96+132\\text{i}-152\\text{i}-209{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{8}^{2}-(11\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{96-20\\text{i}-209(-1)}{{8}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{96-20\\text{i}+209}{64+121} \\\\  & =\\frac{305-20\\text{i}}{185} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(61-4\\text{i})}{5\\cdot 37} \\\\  & =\\frac{61-4\\text{i}}{37}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{61-4\\text{i}}{37}=(\\frac{61}{37})+(-\\frac{4}{37})\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297100 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297552 1069 "q-316761" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297552 1071 "Let $r=⟨-5,1,3⟩$ and $s=⟨n,-2,4⟩.$ Given that the projection of $r$ onto $s$ is the zero vector, find the value of $n.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297552 1072 #uuid "10cb6fed-87b6-5198-aad0-5655fbefff1f" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297552 1074 "Since $r$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $s$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\nr⊥s\\,⟹\\,r\\cdot s=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & ={r}_{x}\\cdot {s}_{x}+{r}_{y}\\cdot {s}_{y}+{r}_{z}\\cdot {s}_{z} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot n+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-5n-2+12 \\\\  & =10-5n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot s & =0 \\\\ 10-5n & =0 \\\\ n & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296704 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296895 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186297077 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1069 "q-316768" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1071 "Calculate ${A}^{-1},$ where\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${A}^{-1}=\\,\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1072 #uuid "1c5d479a-db20-5ea3-ab0e-6622e81ae62c" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297553 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(A)=5\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0=-10\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-10}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1/2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1/2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {A}^{-1}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1/5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1/2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296700 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296941 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297554 1069 "q-316773" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297554 1071 "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\text{i}$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297554 1072 #uuid "7b6ea44a-cb33-5d3e-be17-0e5b6ad67140" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true] [17592186297554 1074 "The denominator is $1+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{1+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-3\\text{i}}{1-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6+\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})}{(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-18\\text{i}+\\text{i}-3{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-17\\text{i}-3(-1)}{1-(-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-17\\text{i}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9-17\\text{i}}{10}=(\\frac{9}{10})+(-\\frac{17}{10})\\,\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00628492-eb93-5878-979e-30036d304edf" 17592186295276 "answer-00d74572-6c12-570d-b5b4-46d60457646d" 17592186295277 "answer-021675ce-f69e-5717-beee-62f1b55fe9a1" 17592186295278 "answer-02184017-3e72-54aa-bfde-e944921a8b7b" 17592186295279 "answer-0233f9ce-2764-5d65-ab16-8209abe9223a" 17592186295280 "answer-0265ab4b-e69c-58cd-bb39-877b73f3298f" 17592186295281 "answer-02b95ba0-8776-5e87-8a07-fb4dd6e251e3" 17592186295282 "answer-02ba3c43-03f3-524b-962b-cc47531b0217" 17592186295283 "answer-032db269-5170-5f9b-a293-a64c6b8de5f3" 17592186295284 "answer-0336dc05-5463-5201-a8da-0faecf9910eb" 17592186295285 "answer-036c4578-